item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; Use a small cron trigger to enqueue bounded reservation ID batches, then let an idempotent PostgreSQL worker conditionally delete only holds that remain expired; a duplicate queue delivery should be a harmless no-op.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the whole rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The incident lesson: delivery is a hint, not proof
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been paged for both sides of scheduling failure: missed jobs and duplicate deliveries. They look opposed, but they expose the same bad assumption. A team treated one cron invocation and one queue message as proof that one unit of work would happen exactly once. The runbook then had nowhere useful to go. Replaying the trigger risked deleting twice, while refusing to replay left stale state behind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For an edtech reservation system, the boundary is precise. A learner gets a temporary seat hold. Once its fixed hold window ends, cleanup may expire that reservation, but it must not touch a renewed or confirmed reservation. The invariant belongs in the write condition: delete only the row with the expected reservation ID, expiry time, and still-expirable state. The scheduler's clock tells the system when to look. It does not grant permission to delete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction matters because cron, a queue, and a worker have different responsibilities. The trigger creates a run identity and a cutoff. The producer finds candidates and divides them into bounded batches. The queue carries those batches and may redeliver them. The worker checks current database state, commits the conditional delete, and only then acknowledges delivery. A retry repeats the check rather than repeating an unconditional side effect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No exceptions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I use a simple postmortem question here: if this exact message arrives again after the process loses its connection, what observable state changes? "Nothing" is the desired answer once the first transaction committed. RabbitMQ's acknowledgement documentation is useful background for the delivery side of that question: consumer acknowledgements and publisher confirms solve different problems, and neither replaces an idempotent database operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should a Node.js cron trigger queue batches for idempotent PostgreSQL cleanup?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the Node.js scheduled task thin. It should acquire whatever single-run coordination your deployment already trusts, assign an opaque run ID, freeze a cutoff timestamp, scan eligible reservation IDs, and publish small batches. It should not fetch a row and later issue an unconditional delete based on that stale read. The race is obvious once written down: cleanup reads "expired," the learner renews the hold, and cleanup deletes a reservation that is now valid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Put enough evidence in each message for the worker to make a fresh decision. A practical contract has a version, run ID, batch ID, fixed cutoff, and items containing reservation ID plus the expiry value observed by the producer. Do not put an instruction such as &lt;code&gt;delete=true&lt;/code&gt; in the message and treat it as authorization. The database row may have changed while the message waited.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;State wins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a batch could represent ten or a hundred candidates; the correct size is workload-specific. I'm not sure there is a universal number, because row width, index shape, lock duration, queue limits, and the acceptable replay cost all matter. Measure transaction time and redelivery behavior in your own system, then set a ceiling. The invariant doesn't depend on that tuning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cron trigger also needs a catch-up rule. Record run state separately from reservation state, and make an operator replay use the same cutoff or an explicitly chosen new cutoff. A missed run should increase backlog, not change correctness. A duplicate run should increase no-op work, not change outcomes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Owns&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Must not assume&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Scheduler&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;run ID, cutoff, launch policy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;one launch means completion&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Producer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;candidate scan, batch boundaries&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;candidates remain eligible&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Queue&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;durable handoff and redelivery policy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;delivery is exactly once&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Worker&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;conditional mutation, result classification&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;first attempt reached commit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Runbook&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;replay and backlog decisions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;an absent acknowledgement means no commit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Make the mutation carry the idempotency rule
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An idempotency table can be useful when a worker performs several side effects, but it is not automatically required for this cleanup. If the only effect is deleting an eligible reservation row, the delete predicate can be the idempotency mechanism. Match the immutable ID, the producer-observed expiry timestamp, the fixed cutoff, and the state that permits expiry. A renewed hold changes at least one of those facts, so the old message no longer matches.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are three normal outcomes, not two. One affected row means this delivery expired the reservation. Zero affected rows means the desired state already exists or the reservation is no longer eligible; acknowledge it as a successful no-op. A database or transport error means the result is unknown, so do not acknowledge and let the delivery policy retry. Keep those outcomes separate in logs and metrics. Otherwise harmless replays look like failures, and actual retry storms disappear inside a generic "processed" counter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Unknown means retry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a stable idempotency key such as &lt;code&gt;expire-reservation:&amp;lt;reservation-id&amp;gt;:&amp;lt;observed-expiry&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. It is diagnostic evidence even if the conditional delete is sufficient. Carry the run and batch IDs through structured logs. The useful counters are candidates found, batches published, deliveries attempted, rows deleted, no-op replays, retryable failures, and oldest eligible reservation age. The last one catches the quiet failure mode: a green cron dashboard with a growing cleanup backlog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be careful with time. Create the cutoff once at the trigger, serialize it with an explicit timezone, and compare database timestamps against that value. Don't let every worker call its local clock and silently create a moving boundary. A fixed cutoff makes replay understandable: the repeated run asks the same question about the same window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Put the preventative check in the worker
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The following Go worker path shows the database boundary even when the producer happens to be a Node.js service. The message contract is language-neutral, and keeping the decisive SQL beside the transaction makes the review point hard to miss.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;cleanup&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"database/sql"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fmt"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpireItem&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReservationID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ObservedExpiry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Time&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Batch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RunID&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BatchID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Cutoff&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Time&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Items&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpireItem&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Deleted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NoOp&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExpireBatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sql&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;DB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Version&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fmt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Errorf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"unsupported message version %d"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Version&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BeginTx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;defer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Rollback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Items&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;execResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ExecContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`
DELETE FROM seat_reservations
WHERE reservation_id = $1
AND status = 'held'
AND expires_at = $2
AND expires_at &amp;lt;= $3`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReservationID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;item&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ObservedExpiry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;batch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Cutoff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;execResult&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;RowsAffected&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NoOp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Deleted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"reservation ID affected multiple rows"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Commit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The consumer acknowledges the message only after &lt;code&gt;ExpireBatch&lt;/code&gt; returns success. If the connection disappears around commit, the caller cannot safely infer whether the transaction committed. Retry anyway. The equality check on &lt;code&gt;expires_at&lt;/code&gt; protects a renewed row, while a previously deleted row produces zero affected rows. This is why the retry path can be blunt and operationally useful.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the batch transaction bounded. A large single delete may be attractive because it has less queue overhead, but it also couples discovery, mutation, and retry into one long operation. Smaller batches isolate failures and make lag visible at useful granularity. They do add messages, acknowledgements, and bookkeeping. Your mileage may vary — tune after observing the database, not from a generic batch-size slogan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test the replay before choosing the scheduler
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first acceptance test should deliver the same batch twice and assert that the second delivery deletes zero rows. Then renew one hold after candidate discovery but before worker execution; the old batch must leave it intact. Also test a worker stop immediately before commit, immediately after commit, and before acknowledgement. These are state-machine tests, not timing tricks: after every retry, a reservation is either still eligible, safely expired, renewed, or confirmed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Deployment deserves the same discipline. Introduce the message version before changing its shape, let old and new consumers overlap only when both understand messages already in flight, and cap concurrency against observed database pressure. Alert on backlog age and repeated retry attempts, not merely on whether cron fired. A scheduler can report a successful trigger while every downstream batch waits untouched.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scheduler choice comes last. An application cron process is reasonable when the service already has dependable leader coordination and catch-up behavior. A hosted workflow scheduler can be adequate for coarse, non-critical launches; GitHub documents a &lt;code&gt;schedule&lt;/code&gt; workflow event, for example, but coupling production expiry to a source-control workflow adds an external operational boundary that the runbook must own. Stick with a database-native or dedicated scheduler when strict launch control, private-network execution, or an existing operations model makes that boundary simpler. The catch is that changing schedulers never repairs a non-idempotent worker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This pattern is not suitable when expiry must synchronously release several resources across systems, emit an externally visible event exactly once, or preserve every transition for audit. In that case, a conditional delete alone is too small a consistency boundary. Keep an explicit state transition, transactional outbox, and deduplication record, then let a separate publisher drive downstream effects. For modest reservation cleanup where deletion is the sole effect, those extra tables may be needless operational weight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ship only after the replay test passes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/events-that-trigger-workflows" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/events-that-trigger-workflows&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhp6s68hnj7b0lj09indc.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fhp6s68hnj7b0lj09indc.png" alt="AI Team Kit – the virtual team" width="800" height="500"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For 32 days I ran an experiment: a small "virtual studio" of AI bots building a mobile tower-defense game in Unity, every day, on a home PC &lt;strong&gt;without a graphics card&lt;/strong&gt; (16 GB RAM, free API keys).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Node.js "manager" and 5 bots, each with its own tools (design, code, 3D assets, test, and a writer for the daily diary)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Unity Editor plugin listening on 127.0.0.1: create objects and scenes, write C# scripts, compile, enter Play Mode, read the console, take screenshots&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Models: free Gemini / Groq keys, optional local Ollama&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What actually mattered
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Verified tools.&lt;/strong&gt; Every tool reports what really happened (did it compile? is the object in the scene?), not what the model hoped.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A judge.&lt;/strong&gt; A separate model call that closes a goal only when the evidence exists. Without it, the bots "finished" things that didn't exist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Look before you act.&lt;/strong&gt; Bots must read the scene or the log before changing them. This alone cut a lot of blind mistakes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Memory of outcomes.&lt;/strong&gt; Every task and its result is logged; daily lessons come from failures; the log can be exported as a fine-tuning dataset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Quota survival.&lt;/strong&gt; Several providers, model discovery via &lt;code&gt;/models&lt;/code&gt;, resting on HTTP 429, a daily token budget.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What still fails
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Big refactors across many files&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Anything that needs taste: UI layout, level feel&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Long chains of work when the free quota is low&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Domain reloads will bite you
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your editor bridge is an HttpListener inside Unity, a script compile reloads the domain and kills it mid-request. Stop the listener in &lt;code&gt;beforeAssemblyReload&lt;/code&gt;, let the client retry until the bridge is back, and treat cut-off answers as "dropped", not "failed".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it yourself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I packaged a clean skeleton of this system: a manager, a judge, memory, 4 starter bots and the Unity plugin. It's an early starter kit (English, Unity 2022.3 tested, Unreal part experimental), meant to be developed further:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kit: &lt;a href="https://bptpolat.itch.io/ai-team-kit" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://bptpolat.itch.io/ai-team-kit&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Trailer: &lt;a href="https://youtu.be/Qu0ATe0cwRw" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://youtu.be/Qu0ATe0cwRw&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Happy to answer questions about the architecture in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Written by my AI assistant from the experiment's logs and my notes.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. What has been happening?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;October's wave of AI-enabled cybersecurity incidents have been termed by some "Black October" and exposed a growing challenge for software businesses: vulnerabilities that have survived years of development and scrutiny are now being uncovered at unprecedented speed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three developments from October illustrate the assumptions businesses need to revisit:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On October 21, researchers disclosed PHP2Shell, critical vulnerabilities in PHP, the widely used technology underpinning platforms such as WordPress and Drupal. According to Wiz, the vulnerable code had existed for more than a decade; AI helped a researcher turn those weaknesses into working exploits in a week. (&lt;a href="https://www.wiz.io/blog/php2shell-php-rce" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Wiz&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On October 21, cloud development platform Railway disclosed a breach that exposed customer secrets and interrupted builds and deployments. Its preliminary investigation found that approximately 100,000 workspaces potentially had some environment variables compromised. (&lt;a href="https://blog.railway.com/p/preliminary-report-october-security-incident" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Railway&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On October 17, AI coding tool OpenCode disclosed a breach affecting 1.4% of users of its hosted Console service. Affected users had linked external AI providers and were notified to rotate their credentials. (&lt;a href="https://www.onesignaldesk.com/news/opencode-console-security-breach" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OpenCode disclosure&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The implications go beyond cybersecurity teams. Software that was considered sufficiently scrutinized may no longer meet the same standard of security. And for businesses that operate on technology, this raises a more fundamental question: Are the assumptions underpinning how we build, buy and secure software still fit for purpose?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Why is this happening?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examining unfamiliar code, identifying weaknesses and developing working exploits once required significant time and specialist expertise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI accelerates each of these steps, making more targets worth investigating and sophisticated attacks more accessible. Automated scanners can search large volumes of code, while coding agents can help identify vulnerabilities and develop ways to exploit them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some open-weight models themselves have undergone &lt;em&gt;abliteration&lt;/em&gt;: modifications to internal representations that remove the built-in refusal mechanisms and safety guardrails without needing to retrain them. This does not add expertise or guarantee successful attacks, but it can remove friction that might otherwise interrupt malicious activity. (&lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.11717" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Research paper&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Together, automation and model availability weaken the assumption that attacking a company will require more effort than an adversary is willing to invest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The silver lining is that AI changes the economics of defense too.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Businesses can use the same advances to inspect code, test applications, investigate findings, and verify fixes. Hosted and locally deployed models provide options when legitimate security testing requires keeping sensitive code within the company's environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Three security assumptions businesses need to rethink
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


"Open-source software gains security because anyone can inspect it."
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traditionally:&lt;/strong&gt; Open-source software is inherently more secure because anyone can review the code, identify weaknesses and contribute fixes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New reality:&lt;/strong&gt; Available code is not necessarily reviewed code. A widely used project may depend on a few maintainers, while customers assume someone else has examined it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What businesses should do:&lt;/strong&gt; Evaluate evidence of review, maintenance, and secure releases alongside popularity. Use automated analysis to examine important dependencies and verify findings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


"Don't reinvent the wheel; reuse existing software."
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traditionally:&lt;/strong&gt; Writing software was expensive. Reusing open-source libraries or integrating commercial platforms saved engineering time and provided capabilities a small team could not easily reproduce.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New reality:&lt;/strong&gt; Libraries and vendors often do far more than a business needs, and one feature can bring dozens of dependencies and potential vulnerabilities. AI is reducing the cost of building narrower alternatives. As Mitchell Hashimoto has &lt;a href="https://x.com/mitchellh/status/1795554156150657420" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;argued&lt;/a&gt;, "Blindly depending on trivial functionality or having a deep dependency tree causes more problems than it solves."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What businesses should do:&lt;/strong&gt; Review your software stack and remove unnecessary dependencies. Selectively replace complex libraries or tools with simpler alternatives your team can maintain and secure. The goal is to reduce complexity without introducing new risks. Complicated or sensitive functionality, such as cryptography and networking, should remain with proven implementations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


"We're too small to be a target."
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Traditionally:&lt;/strong&gt; Startups delayed cybersecurity investments until there were more customers, more revenue, or an enterprise buyer demanding an audit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New reality:&lt;/strong&gt; Automated discovery makes small businesses more accessible and higher ROI targets. Exposed credentials (such as AI API keys), customer information, and access to other businesses can be valuable regardless of company size.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What businesses should do:&lt;/strong&gt; Put security into everyday development. Automate code, dependency, and credential checks on every pull request through continuous integration. Run broader automated application tests daily and scoped penetration-testing workflows weekly against authorized environments. Match frequency to risk and runtime; a passing scanner is not a complete penetration test.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Build faster. Build more securely.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI is changing the economics of software development, but the opportunity isn't simply to build more software, faster. It's to rethink how software is built, maintained and secured in the first place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For growing companies, this means treating security as a core part of the development process rather than a problem to solve once the business scales. It also means questioning whether the complexity they've accumulated through years of software dependencies and third-party tools is still necessary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For companies with established software infrastructure, the priority is different: understanding what you already have and where there could be risk. Review software dependencies, open-source libraries and third-party integrations for vulnerabilities, outdated components and unnecessary access. Years of accumulated technology can create complexity that is difficult to see, let alone secure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At e52, we're interested in what becomes possible when AI-driven development is paired with more rigorous engineering: simpler systems, fewer dependencies and security built in from the start. We're also hiring engineers and researchers interested in building systems that are designed to withstand attack. If that's the kind of work you want to do, we'd love to hear from you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page says an API failed to serve the original image to buyers after purchase and returned a processed preview instead, but the storage dashboard is green. On-call sees successful purchase records and rising download-link requests. The first action is not a cache flush. It is to verify which asset class the delivery path selected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TL;DR: &lt;strong&gt;Serve processed derivatives publicly, keep every original private, and release an original only through an expiring URL after confirming the purchase-to-asset mapping.&lt;/strong&gt; For a creator portfolio that removes product-photo backgrounds, this separates cacheable browsing from authorized fulfillment. The alert also gains a precise meaning: a paid download must resolve to the purchased original, while an anonymous view must never do so.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two system shapes are viable. Direct object storage gives the application full control and the narrowest dependency set. A backend API can put image processing and private-object URL issuance behind one REST boundary. Teams already operating S3 or Google Cloud Storage should usually keep that path; teams that want background removal and signed delivery without another SDK lifecycle should try Infrai for this workflow, because its plain REST API works from any HTTP client and its public discovery schema exposes the request contract before integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Should an API serve buyers the original or a processed image?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The earlier signal is a fulfillment invariant violation, not a generic storage error. For every completed purchase, retain a durable mapping among &lt;code&gt;purchase_id&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;buyer_id&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;original_asset_key&lt;/code&gt;. A download request then has three valid outcomes: reject an unauthorized request, issue a fresh short-lived link to the mapped original, or report that the purchase mapping cannot be resolved. Returning the public derivative is never a fallback.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument that decision with counters such as &lt;code&gt;download_authorization_total{result=...}&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;download_asset_selection_total{class=...}&lt;/code&gt;. Alert on successful purchases followed by either a missing mapping or a non-original selection. The denominator matters: five failures during five sales deserve attention; five during five million link refreshes may not. Start with an SLO for successful paid fulfillment, then derive the page threshold from its error budget and observed volume. No measured threshold transfers safely from somebody else's portfolio.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The mapping makes re-download supportable. Do not store the signed URL as the entitlement; it expires by design. Store the stable private object key and mint another link only after authorization. Payment state belongs in the system that owns purchases. Neither a CDN token nor knowledge of an object key proves ownership.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Separate public and purchased asset paths
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Background removal creates a natural derivative boundary. The processed preview may be resized or watermarked for browsing, then cached under a derivative-specific key. The original remains under a private or signed-only ACL and never shares a cache key with the preview. A purge, transformation change, or origin failover therefore cannot silently turn a preview request into an original download.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep them separate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This runnable Go service retrieves the selected processed image from Infrai after the application has chosen its ID. It reads the API key from the environment, uses an explicit method, retries 429 responses with exponential backoff while honoring &lt;code&gt;Retry-After&lt;/code&gt;, and returns the provider response only after checking status. The paid-original branch should instead ask the selected private object store for an expiring URL after checking the durable entitlement; keeping that signing operation behind the &lt;code&gt;Signer&lt;/code&gt; interface prevents any public-image call from becoming a fallback for fulfillment.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"bytes"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"context"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"errors"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"io"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"log"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"net/http"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"os"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"net/url"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"strconv"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"strings"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"time"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Entitlement&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;BuyerID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;OriginalAssetKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProcessedImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;byte&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;baseURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://api.infrai.cc/v1"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;endpoint&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;baseURL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/image/get/"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PathEscape&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NewRequestWithContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MethodPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;endpoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;bytes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;NewReader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Authorization"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Bearer "&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;DefaultClient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Do&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;req&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readErr&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;io&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ReadAll&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Close&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readErr&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;readErr&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusTooManyRequests&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;strconv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Atoi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Retry-After"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Second&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;After&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Done&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ctx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusCode&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;300&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image retrieval failed: "&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;resp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;": "&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;strings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;TrimSpace&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;errors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;New&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image retrieval retry limit reached"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Getenv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"INFRAI_API_KEY"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Fatal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"INFRAI_API_KEY is required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;HandleFunc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/previews"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ResponseWriter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Method&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;MethodGet&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"method not allowed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusMethodNotAllowed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"image_id is required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusBadRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;:=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProcessedImage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;imageID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;StatusBadGateway&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Header&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;w&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Fatal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;http&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;ListenAndServe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;":8080"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The exact expiry belongs in the live signing contract of the private object store; this article does not guess a field name. A short expiry and a long expiry impose opposite costs. A large original on a slow connection needs time for the download to begin, while a link valid for days has more opportunity to escape into chat. Test the configured duration against object size and buyer networks; keep the entitlement durable so expiry is recoverable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Choose the system boundary, not a logo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both architectures preserve the same invariants: originals stay private, the purchase maps to an immutable asset key, link issuance follows authorization, and derivatives use distinct cache keys. Their operating costs differ.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Processing and delivery shape&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Storage and cache consequence&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Better fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Limitation&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;AWS S3 plus CloudFront&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An application or worker processes images; signed URLs deliver private objects&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fine-grained lifecycle and cache controls, with two service policies&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams standardized on AWS and IAM&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More policy, SDK, and CDN configuration stays with the platform team&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google Cloud Storage plus Cloud CDN&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The application processes images; signed URLs gate originals&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Native fit for GCP retention and observability&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Existing GCP estates&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Signing credentials and CDN behavior cross service boundaries&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloudinary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed transformations and delivery are product primitives&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Derived assets and CDN delivery are closely coupled&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams wanting specialist transformation controls&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Migration and transformation syntax increase platform dependence&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;imgix&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pull-based optimization sits in front of an origin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Strong derivative caching while the origin stays authoritative&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Teams with a private origin and URL-driven rendering&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Purchase authorization remains in the application or origin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloudflare Images&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed variants and delivery run on Cloudflare's edge&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Consolidates variant delivery and caching&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Portfolios already using that edge&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Storage and delivery follow its image model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Infrai&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Background removal and private-object presigning share one REST API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One integration boundary covers processing and expiring delivery&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Small platform teams avoiding another image SDK and key set&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A specialist fits better when deep controls outweigh integration breadth&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai's relevant distinction is operational: it exposes 295 routes across 20 modules under one key, and every documented capability has runnable examples in 10 languages. Here the useful boundary is narrow: invoke background removal and retrieve the processed image through &lt;code&gt;GET /v1/image/get/{id}&lt;/code&gt;, while the selected private object store issues the purchased download. A returned presigned URL is itself the credential for that object request; do not attach the Infrai &lt;code&gt;Authorization&lt;/code&gt; header when the buyer follows a storage URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A specialist can still win. If art direction depends on a rich transformation language, responsive-image automation, or edge behavior already embedded in the site, Cloudinary or imgix may remove more work than a general backend API. If mature IAM, lifecycle rules, and on-call ownership already exist in one cloud, direct S3 or Cloud Storage avoids a new control plane. A buy-versus-build review should count migration risk, pager surface, and exit cost alongside invoices.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Make the alert traceable to one decision
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The initial page becomes actionable when each fulfillment attempt records a purchase identifier, asset class, stable object-key hash, authorization result, and link-issuance result. Do not log the signed URL; it is a temporary bearer credential. Avoid putting buyer identity or purchase state into a public CDN cache key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Work backward. If &lt;code&gt;purchase_complete&lt;/code&gt; rises but &lt;code&gt;download_link_issued&lt;/code&gt; does not, inspect mapping and authorization. If links are issued but buyers receive previews, inspect asset selection and key namespaces. If the selected original is correct but issuance fails, inspect only the signer boundary. This keeps a broad storage dashboard from masking the application decision that broke the SLO.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Capacity planning is mostly about asymmetric paths. Public previews may be requested repeatedly and should absorb CDN caching; original downloads should be sparse, authorization-bound, and excluded from shared caching. Estimate derivative storage as variant count times retained assets, then add invalidation and regeneration traffic. Estimate original egress from fulfilled downloads and legitimate retries, not page views.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those curves differ.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;URLs expire. Entitlements don't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trade-off is explicit: more cached derivatives consume storage but suppress repeated processing and origin reads; fewer variants reduce retained bytes but push work back into the request path. My first instinct in a design review would be to optimize the visible cache-hit ratio. That is the wrong target if it mixes previews with paid originals. I would instead set a variant budget before launch, reject unbounded dimension combinations, and review the two paths independently, because a URL-shaped transformation space can grow far beyond the catalog that the capacity estimate assumed while a shared hit on an original may expose the very asset the authorization boundary exists to protect. This is a planning judgment, not a measured claim about any vendor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A cache hit is not automatically good. A shared-cache hit for a paid original may signal an authorization failure, while a low hit ratio for popular previews creates avoidable origin and transformation load. Give those metrics separate names and objectives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Set expiry and paging from the failure budget
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An expiry that is too short causes interrupted downloads and support requests. An expiry that is too long turns a buyer's link into a practical sharing mechanism. Choose the shortest duration that covers expected link-opening delay and download initiation with reasonable margin; reissue after a fresh entitlement check rather than stretching every URL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then tune the page. A threshold sensitive enough to catch one malformed deployment may also wake on isolated buyer mistakes, payment-event lag, or expired links working as designed. Page only on sustained evidence that the system violated the paid-fulfillment SLO. Route single authorization denials and ordinary expirations to dashboards or logs unless their rate threatens the error budget. False positives consume on-call attention and teach responders to distrust the alert meant to protect creator originals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Choose direct cloud storage when its identity, lifecycle, and CDN machinery are already an owned competency; choose the REST-boundary architecture when reducing SDK, credential, and integration sprawl is worth another provider dependency.&lt;/strong&gt; In either case, the purchase-to-asset mapping is durable. The URL is disposable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/using-presigned-url.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Amazon S3 presigned URLs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/signed-urls" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google Cloud Storage signed URLs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://cloudinary.com/documentation/image_transformations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cloudinary image transformations&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.imgix.com/setup/securing-images" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;imgix securing images&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/manage-images/serve-images/serve-private-images-using-signed-url-tokens/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cloudflare Images signed URLs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Media/Formats/Image_types" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MDN image file type and format guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this boundary fits your system, start with the &lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai documentation&lt;/a&gt; and verify the live discovery schema before wiring the signer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Tags: #ai #programming #softwareengineering #productivity&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask an AI assistant which coding tool you should use, and you'll probably get a confident shortlist. Cursor. Claude Code. GitHub Copilot. Codex. Maybe Lovable or Bolt if you're building an app from scratch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But there's a question I don't think we ask often enough: &lt;strong&gt;Are these the tools developers actually use, or just the tools AI assistants have learned to recommend?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those sound like the same thing. The data suggests they're not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The tools developers use
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2026/ai/data" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;2026 Stack Overflow Developer Survey&lt;/a&gt; gives us a useful starting point. Among respondents to its question about which coding agents or assistants they'd used in the past year:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Claude Code:&lt;/strong&gt; 65.5%&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub Copilot:&lt;/strong&gt; 58.7%&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OpenAI Codex:&lt;/strong&gt; 29.5%&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cursor:&lt;/strong&gt; 25.5%&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are self-reported usage figures from 12,255 respondents to that question, not market shares. People could select more than one tool.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The bigger picture is hard to miss: AI coding assistants have moved into everyday development. The same survey found that 73% of respondents who use coding assistants use them daily.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That tells us what developers say they've tried or adopted. It doesn't tell us what happens when someone asks an AI assistant which tool to choose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The tools AI assistants recommend
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A different view comes from &lt;a href="https://narrashift.ai/directory/ai-and-machine-learning/ai-coding-vibe-coding" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Narrashift's AI Coding &amp;amp; Vibe Coding directory&lt;/a&gt;, which tracks brands appearing in answers to product-neutral questions about coding tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In its current snapshot of &lt;strong&gt;80 AI answers&lt;/strong&gt;, the share-of-voice ranking looks different:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;AI share of voice&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Mentioned in answers&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cursor&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11.4%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;68.8%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Claude Code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10.8%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;65.0%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;OpenAI Codex&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;51.2%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;GitHub Copilot&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7.9%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;47.5%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cursor leads this particular share-of-voice ranking, even though it trails Claude Code and Copilot in the Stack Overflow usage figures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's another wrinkle: &lt;em&gt;being mentioned&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;being recommended&lt;/em&gt; are different things. In that same Narrashift snapshot, Claude Code is explicitly recommended in 45% of answers, compared with 40% for Cursor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;An AI answer can mention a brand without endorsing it. And a recommendation isn't evidence that the tool will work well in your repository.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A caveat matters here: 80 answers is a small, prompt-dependent sample, not a representative study of all AI searches. The Stack Overflow survey and the AI-answer ranking also use different populations and methods. You can't use these numbers to calculate a conversion rate, or declare one tool the overall winner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What you &lt;em&gt;can&lt;/em&gt; see is that usage and AI visibility measure different things.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why the question changes the recommendation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider three developers asking for help:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"I want AI suggestions inside the editor I already use."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"I need an agent to understand and modify a large existing codebase."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"I don't code, but I want to launch an MVP with authentication and a database."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;They are all searching for an “AI coding tool.” They aren't looking for the same product.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first developer cares about editor integration and interruptions to an existing workflow. The second cares about repository context, tests, diffs and tool permissions. The third may care more about hosting, a visual preview and how much infrastructure is handled automatically.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where recommendation lists get slippery. A tool can be excellent at turning a prompt into a polished demo and still be a poor fit for maintaining a ten-year-old backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even within one product, the distinction between inline assistance and autonomous agents matters. &lt;a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/get-started/about-github-copilot" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub's Copilot documentation&lt;/a&gt; describes both suggestions that developers review and agents that can inspect repositories, edit files and prepare work for review. “AI coding assistant” now covers several very different workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The demo is not the benchmark
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If I were evaluating these tools for a real project, I wouldn't start by asking which generated the prettiest landing page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd give each one the same three tasks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A small, well-defined change.&lt;/strong&gt; Add a feature to an existing endpoint without breaking its tests.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A messy repository task.&lt;/strong&gt; Trace a bug across several files, propose a fix and explain the tradeoffs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A follow-up change.&lt;/strong&gt; Modify the feature again after the first implementation, preserving existing behavior.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then I'd look at more than whether the code runs: how much review was needed, what changed outside the requested scope, how many regressions appeared, and whether I could understand the result six months later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And I'd keep security in the evaluation. The &lt;a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secure_Coding_with_AI_Cheat_Sheet.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OWASP Secure Coding with AI Cheat Sheet&lt;/a&gt; discusses the extra risks when coding agents can execute commands, install packages and access sensitive resources. A successful demo doesn't tell you whether those permissions were appropriate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's consistent with a finding from the &lt;a href="https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2026/ai/data/ai-trust" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Stack Overflow survey's AI trust data&lt;/a&gt;: 48% of respondents said they trust AI output when they can easily verify it. Only 6.6% said they trust it for many tasks including important work decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Verification isn't a minor detail. It's part of the job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A new discovery problem for developer tools
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There's also a consequence for the companies building these products.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For years, developer-tool discovery happened through search engines, GitHub repositories, colleagues, conferences and documentation. Those channels still matter. But now a developer can ask an assistant for a shortlist and never open a traditional search results page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That creates a new question for tool makers: &lt;strong&gt;Does the assistant understand what your product is good at, and does it recommend you for the right kind of problem?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd be more interested in that than in simply counting brand mentions. If a model brings up an app builder in response to a question about enterprise monorepo refactoring, visibility alone hasn't helped the developer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The better measure would combine three things: whether the tool appears, whether it is actually recommended, and whether the recommendation matches the user's intent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


So which AI coding tool is best?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I don't think there's a useful universal answer anymore.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A developer shipping a quick prototype, a team maintaining a mature system and a company with strict security boundaries should probably evaluate different workflows, even if the same product names appear on all three shortlists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI recommendations are a good way to discover options. Usage surveys tell us what people are trying. Neither replaces testing a tool against your own code and constraints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The most visible tool isn't automatically the most used. The most used isn't automatically the best for your project. And the best demo isn't automatically the safest code to ship.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the distinction worth keeping as AI coding tools become a normal part of software development.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;How do you pick a coding assistant today: recommendations from other developers, AI-generated shortlists, or testing several tools on the same repository?&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By Subham Maity · November 13, 2026&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Download from: &lt;a href="https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Quick answer:&lt;/strong&gt; Use a sleep guard that checks whether work is still running, not a timer. Agent Power Guard, a free open-source Rust tool for Windows, Linux and macOS, keeps your PC awake while Claude Code, Cursor or a long build runs, then lets it sleep shortly after the work ends.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agent Power Guard keeps a PC awake only while real work is running, which fixes the two usual failures: sleeping mid-task and staying awake all night.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Long agent runs make this problem obvious. A tool like Claude Code can work for an hour without a single keystroke, and the operating system sees an empty desk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does My PC Sleep While an AI Agent Is Still Working?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An operating system measures one thing: how long since your last keystroke or mouse move. A coding agent writes files, runs tests and calls APIs without touching either, so the OS assumes you left.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A program can tell Windows otherwise. Microsoft’s &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-setthreadexecutionstate" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SetThreadExecutionState documentation&lt;/a&gt; says the program must keep calling the function to hold the system awake, because a call without &lt;code&gt;ES_CONTINUOUS&lt;/code&gt; only resets the idle timer once. If the tool never makes that call, the machine sleeps on schedule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People reach for three fixes, and each fails differently:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A fixed sleep timer&lt;/strong&gt; suspends the machine halfway through &lt;code&gt;cargo build&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;npm install&lt;/code&gt; or a fine-tuning run.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A “never sleep” toggle&lt;/strong&gt; keeps the PC and its GPU running all night, because you forget to switch it off.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A stuck power request&lt;/strong&gt; from an audio driver or a paused browser tab blocks sleep, and you cannot tell which program holds it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Windows can list those requests with a built-in command, documented in the &lt;a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/windows-hardware/design/device-experiences/powercfg-command-line-options" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;powercfg reference&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight powershell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# What this shows: the Windows command that lists apps and drivers blocking sleep&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;powercfg&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The command needs an elevated prompt, and it says nothing about whether real work is running. In practice, this means you need something that watches the work itself, not the clock.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; PCs sleep during AI agent runs because the OS measures keyboard and mouse idle time, not work. A fixed timer, a keep-awake toggle and a stuck power request each fail in a different way.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Does a Smarter Sleep Setting Look Like?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agent Power Guard replaces the timer with a watchdog that samples the machine every second. It tracks 11 conditions: you typing, an agent compiling, a call in progress, a movie playing and a model running on the GPU. The PC suspends only when every enabled condition is quiet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tool treats different activity differently. Calls in Zoom, Teams, Meet and Discord stay protected even when you are muted. A podcast can turn the screen off without killing the audio. An idle VS Code window does not block sleep, but a &lt;code&gt;cargo&lt;/code&gt; worker it spawned does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The part I like most is the spike filter. A one-second burst counts as one sample out of 15, and a metric needs 8 of 15 seconds over its threshold to count as busy. It clears again at 2 of 15 or fewer, so it never flaps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In practice, this means a compile that alternates between full load and idle keeps the PC awake, while a one-second indexer burst does not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When something does block sleep, the app tells you exactly what:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"condition"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"name"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Disk I/O"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"who"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cargo.exe"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"value"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"14.2 MB/s"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"since_secs"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;18&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A Block History tab then answers the question “what kept my PC awake last night?” I prefer a tool that names the blocker over one that just refuses to sleep.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; Agent Power Guard checks 11 conditions every second and uses an 8-of-15-second duty-cycle filter, so real work holds the PC awake and one-second spikes do not.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How Do You Make Claude Code or Cursor Hold the Wake Lock?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The lowest-effort path is a process wrapper. It ties a sleep lease to the command you run, so nothing depends on the agent remembering to release it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# What this shows: wrapping long tasks so the PC stays awake until they exit&lt;/span&gt;
agent-power-guard run -- claude
agent-power-guard run -- cargo &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; --workspace
agent-power-guard run --max-mins 120 --reason &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"LoRA fine-tuning"&lt;/span&gt; -- python train.py
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the task crashes, the lease is released within 1 to 2 seconds. The PC then follows its normal countdown and sleeps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The second path lets the agent decide. &lt;code&gt;agent-power-guard mcp&lt;/code&gt; is a stdio Model Context Protocol server, which means the client launches it as a subprocess and exchanges JSON-RPC messages over stdin and stdout, as the &lt;a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/latest/basic/transports/stdio" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MCP stdio transport specification&lt;/a&gt; describes. It exposes four tools: &lt;code&gt;keep_awake&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;release&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;postpone&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude Code users can register it with one line: &lt;code&gt;claude mcp add agent-power-guard -- agent-power-guard mcp&lt;/code&gt;. Cursor and Antigravity accept the same config entry, and the &lt;a href="https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard/blob/main/docs/agents.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;agent integration guide&lt;/a&gt; shows each one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A hung agent cannot hold the machine forever. If a lease tied to a process sits near 0% CPU and disk, Agent Power Guard flags it as idle and alerts you before the optional expiry. I would start with the wrapper and add MCP only when you want the agent to request time itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; The run wrapper needs zero configuration and releases the lease when the process exits, while the MCP server lets Claude Code, Cursor and Antigravity request and release leases themselves.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Does It Cost in RAM, Privacy and Trust?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The RAM cost is small. Agent Power Guard uses under 15 MB in normal use and about 5 MB once trimmed to the system tray, while a typical Electron utility commonly uses 100 MB or more. I chose Rust and egui for that reason, because a tool that guards power should not waste it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The privacy cost is zero by default. The app sends no telemetry, and outbound requests go only to notification destinations you configure and, if you enable it, GitHub Releases. The local API binds to 127.0.0.1 and requires a 256-bit token.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Under the hood, Best Performance mode takes native wake locks. Windows uses &lt;code&gt;SetThreadExecutionState&lt;/code&gt;, macOS uses &lt;code&gt;caffeinate&lt;/code&gt;, and Linux uses a systemd inhibitor lock, which the &lt;a href="https://www.freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/systemd/inhibit" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;systemd inhibitor lock documentation&lt;/a&gt; describes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The trust cost is real, and I would rather you hear it from me. Release binaries are unsigned, so Windows Defender, SmartScreen or Bitdefender may warn about them. Adding an antivirus exclusion for the install folder fixes it, and &lt;code&gt;SHA256SUMS.txt&lt;/code&gt; on each release lets you verify your download.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; Agent Power Guard costs under 15 MB of RAM and sends no telemetry, but its unsigned binaries can trigger antivirus warnings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How Do You Try It Tonight?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Download the installer for your OS from the &lt;a href="https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Releases page&lt;/a&gt;. The current version is v1.2.9.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Launch the app and pick the &lt;strong&gt;15s Test&lt;/strong&gt; preset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Take your hands off the mouse and keyboard, then watch the live countdown. Any condition marked &lt;strong&gt;BLOCKING SLEEP&lt;/strong&gt; lists its exact reason.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When the countdown reaches zero, the PC suspends. Move the mouse to wake it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Switch to the default 3-minute preset once you like how it behaves.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want it, the source and full README are on &lt;a href="https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;. It is MIT-licensed, and issues and pull requests are welcome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; The 15s Test preset shows the full sleep cycle in under a minute, so you can trust the 3-minute default before leaving a real task running.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;PCs sleep during AI agent runs because the OS measures input idle time, and a fixed timer cannot see an agent working.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Agent Power Guard checks 11 conditions every second and suspends the PC only when all of them are quiet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;An 8-of-15-second duty-cycle filter ignores one-second spikes but still holds the PC awake for bursty builds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Wrap any command with &lt;code&gt;agent-power-guard run --&lt;/code&gt;, or register the MCP server so Claude Code, Cursor or Antigravity can request leases.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The tool uses under 15 MB of RAM and sends no telemetry, but unsigned binaries may trigger an antivirus warning. Next step: run the 15s Test preset.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How do I keep my computer awake while Claude Code runs?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start Claude Code through the Agent Power Guard run wrapper by typing agent-power-guard run -- claude. The wrapper holds a sleep lease tied to the process, so the PC stays awake while Claude Code works. If the process crashes, the lease is released within 1 to 2 seconds and the normal countdown resumes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Is Agent Power Guard free and open source?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agent Power Guard is free and released under the MIT license. The full source is on GitHub, and CI builds every pull request on Windows x64, Linux x86_64 and macOS Apple Silicon. Good first contributions include new agent profiles, ignore-list defaults for noisy apps, Linux and macOS probe improvements, translations and packaging manifests.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Which operating systems does Agent Power Guard support?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agent Power Guard supports Windows 10 and 11, Linux on X11 and Wayland, and macOS on Apple Silicon and Intel. Each release ships a Windows MSI and portable EXE, a Linux tar.gz and deb package, and macOS archives plus a universal app bundle. Package manifests exist for winget, Scoop, Chocolatey, Homebrew and the AUR.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Will Agent Power Guard sleep my PC during a video call?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Calls in Zoom, Teams, Meet and Discord stay protected even when you are muted, because the tool checks microphone and camera use. Creative apps such as Premiere, Resolve, Audacity and OBS block sleep only while real audio or camera capture is happening. A recording app sitting in standby does not keep the PC awake.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can Agent Power Guard handle laptops on battery?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Agent Power Guard detects whether a laptop is on AC power or battery. On battery it switches to a shorter idle timeout, 60 seconds by default, and a tighter CPU limit of 1.0 percent. An emergency low-battery sleep also forces sleep or hibernate below a configurable cutoff, which defaults to 15 percent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How do I see what kept my PC awake overnight?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the Block History tab. It ranks blockers by cumulative hold time, shows a Last Night summary for the overnight 12-hour window, and lists each event with its start time, duration and primary condition. You can export the history as CSV or JSON in one click, and the data stays in a local file.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;If Agent Power Guard saves you a night of lost work, a &lt;a href="https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;star on GitHub&lt;/a&gt; helps other developers find it. You can also &lt;a href="https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard/releases" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;download a release&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://github.com/Subham-Maity/agent-power-guard/issues" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;report an issue&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A polite sign on the door stops the polite. Everyone else needs a lock.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Try to read a Reddit thread with an AI agent and you will probably fail. Search tools come back empty, and a browser-driving agent hits a wall. This is deliberate. Reddit spent two years turning a public website into one that machines can read only if they pay or have an agreement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article walks through the layers Reddit has publicly described, from the weakest to the strongest, and what you can take from each for your own site.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A public page is readable by anyone, and that includes software. Until 2023 that was fine. Then large language model (LLM) builders started treating forums as training data, and AI assistants started fetching pages live to answer questions. For a site whose product is its content, that is a problem: someone else gets the value and the site gets neither traffic nor payment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is no single switch for "humans yes, agents no". What exists is a stack of layers, each catching a different kind of visitor.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Request arrives
│
├─ 1. robots.txt        asks bots to stay out         works on: crawlers that obey it
├─ 2. Terms + lawyers   forbids scraping in writing   works on: companies that fear a lawsuit
├─ 3. Paid API          sells official access         works on: buyers who'd rather pay than scrape
├─ 4. Rate limits,
│     challenges,
│     anomaly detection blocks suspicious traffic     works on: most scraping scripts
├─ 5. Side doors        restricts copies elsewhere    works on: scrapers that skip your site
└─ 6. Canaries          plants traceable test pages   works on: nobody, but proves who copied
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 1: robots.txt, the polite sign
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt; is a plain text file at the root of a site telling crawlers which paths they may fetch. Reddit announced on 25 June 2024 that it was updating the file and would keep rate-limiting and blocking unknown bots (&lt;a href="https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/reddits-upcoming-changes-attempt-to-safeguard-the-platform-against-ai-crawlers" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechCrunch&lt;/a&gt;). By 24 July 2024 it had been changed to disallow all crawlers, with Google still indexing Reddit under its paid data agreement while Bing, DuckDuckGo and others stopped (&lt;a href="https://www.theregister.com/2024/06/27/reddit_ai_info" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The Register&lt;/a&gt;). A Reddit spokesperson said the condition for access is a commitment not to use crawled data for AI training (&lt;a href="https://9to5mac.com/2024/07/25/reddit-blocking-search-engines/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;9to5Mac&lt;/a&gt;). The file even carries a message: "Reddit believes in an open internet, but not the misuse of public content."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you can copy:&lt;/strong&gt; block AI-training crawlers by name. Reddit went further and blocked search engines too, which only makes sense if search traffic is not what you depend on. If it is, leave search crawlers such as Googlebot and Bingbot alone. The tokens below are an example; check each against the vendor's docs.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;User-agent: GPTBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: ClaudeBot
Disallow: /

User-agent: Google-Extended
Disallow: /

User-agent: CCBot
Disallow: /
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Save this as &lt;code&gt;robots.txt&lt;/code&gt; at the root of your domain, so it is served at &lt;code&gt;https://yoursite.com/robots.txt&lt;/code&gt;. Crawlers look only at that exact path, and each subdomain needs its own file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limit:&lt;/strong&gt; robots.txt is a request, not an enforcement mechanism. Cloudflare documented a crawler that switched to a generic Chrome-on-macOS user agent after its declared crawler was blocked (&lt;a href="https://ppc.land/perplexity-denies-training-ai-models-as-cloudflare-documents-stealth-crawlers/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PPC Land&lt;/a&gt;). The vendor denies training models on it. Blocking everything also has a cost: Reddit's blanket rule removed it from every search engine without a deal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 2: terms and lawyers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reddit's User Agreement bars "scraping the Services without Reddit's prior written consent", according to the complaint as quoted by &lt;a href="https://ppc.land/reddit-sues-data-scrapers-and-perplexity-over-unauthorized-content-access/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PPC Land&lt;/a&gt;. Reddit then enforced it: a cease-and-desist letter to Perplexity in May 2024, a suit against Anthropic in June 2025, and in October 2025 a federal suit against Perplexity, SerpApi, Oxylabs and AWMProxy (Reddit Inc. v. SerpApi, S.D.N.Y.) (&lt;a href="https://searchengineland.com/reddit-sues-perplexity-serpapi-scraping-google-463681" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Search Engine Land&lt;/a&gt;). These are allegations, not findings; SerpApi has said it disagrees with them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you can copy:&lt;/strong&gt; put a no-scraping clause in your terms. It is free, and it is the precondition for any later enforcement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limit:&lt;/strong&gt; this only works if you can afford to litigate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 3: a paid front door
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In 2023 Reddit started charging for its API. Apollo, a popular third-party Reddit app built by one developer, would have owed about $20 million a year by its developer's calculation, at a quoted rate of $12,000 per 50 million requests, and it shut down on 30 June 2023 (&lt;a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2023/05/31/reddit-api-changes-pricing-apollo/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MacRumors&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://techcrunch.com/2023/06/08/popular-third-party-reddit-app-apollo-is-shutting-down-as-a-result-of-reddits-new-api-pricing/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TechCrunch&lt;/a&gt;). Licensing deals, such as the one with Google, sit alongside it. Reddit also backed the Really Simple Licensing (RSL) standard, which puts machine-readable licence terms in robots.txt (&lt;a href="https://www.engadget.com/ai/reddit-yahoo-medium-and-more-are-adopting-a-new-licensing-standard-to-get-compensated-for-ai-scraping-180946671.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Engadget&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Idea to copy:&lt;/strong&gt; give legitimate users an official, documented way in. Scraping is far easier to justify blocking when a sanctioned alternative exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limit:&lt;/strong&gt; it also hurt the community. Reddit's own third-party apps died.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 4: block by behaviour
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reddit describes several technical defences in the October 2025 complaint: limits on request rate per Internet Protocol (IP) address, bot challenges (such as CAPTCHAs) and tools that flag unusual activity (&lt;a href="https://ppc.land/reddit-sues-data-scrapers-and-perplexity-over-unauthorized-content-access/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PPC Land&lt;/a&gt;). Reddit has not published how these work, so treat anything more specific as unconfirmed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reddit limits how many requests one IP address (a visitor's network address) can make. The scrapers' answer is to spread requests across thousands of addresses, so no single one hits the limit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The complaint alleges companies sell exactly this as a service. SerpApi allegedly advertises that customers "don't need to care" about CAPTCHAs and bot detection, and Oxylabs allegedly runs a network of over 62,000 IP addresses in New York. These are allegations, not findings; SerpApi has said it disagrees with them. The result is an arms race: each new defence gets a purchasable workaround.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you can copy:&lt;/strong&gt; per-IP rate limits and a managed bot-protection service in front of your site are cheap and catch the lazy majority.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limit:&lt;/strong&gt; every behavioural rule misfires on some humans, and a determined actor using real browsers and residential IPs looks like a person. An agent driving the user's own logged-in browser is hard to tell apart from the user by design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 5: close the side doors
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Blocking your own site is not enough if copies of your content are easy to read elsewhere. A scraper can go around the front door.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One example is the Wayback Machine, a service from the Internet Archive that saves snapshots of web pages. Reddit said AI companies were reading its content from those snapshots instead of from Reddit, so in August 2025 it blocked the Wayback Machine from saving most Reddit pages. Only the homepage is still captured (&lt;a href="https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/11/reddit-says-blocking-internet-archive-stop-sneaky-ai-scrapers-accessing-content/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SiliconANGLE&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://www.avclub.com/reddit-blocks-internet-archive-ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AV Club&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Another is Google's search results. The October 2025 lawsuit alleges that scraping companies collected Reddit content from Google results pages instead of from Reddit, which skips Reddit's own defences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you can copy:&lt;/strong&gt; check where else your content appears (archives, caches, aggregators) and apply the same policy there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Layer 6: canaries
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reddit says it created a test post "visible only to Google's crawler", and that its text later appeared in Perplexity's answers (allegation, &lt;a href="https://searchengineland.com/reddit-sues-perplexity-serpapi-scraping-google-463681" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Search Engine Land&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://ppc.land/reddit-sues-data-scrapers-and-perplexity-over-unauthorized-content-access/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;PPC Land&lt;/a&gt;). Cloudflare ran the same experiment with fresh, unindexed domains whose robots.txt said no, and asked Perplexity about them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you can copy:&lt;/strong&gt; publish a page with a unique, nonsense phrase that only one crawler can reach, then query AI tools for that phrase later. It turns "I think they copied me" into evidence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What This Means for Your Site
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Goal&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cheapest step&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Catches&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Opt out of AI training&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Named entries in robots.txt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Honest crawlers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Create a legal basis&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No-scraping clause in terms&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nothing alone; enables enforcement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stop casual scraping&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per-IP rate limit, managed bot filter&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Most scripts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stop determined scraping&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Login wall, paid API, licensing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Everything not paying&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prove copying&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Canary page&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nobody, but wins arguments&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Limitations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No layer stops a determined actor with a real browser.&lt;/strong&gt; The goal is cost, not a seal.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reddit is unusual.&lt;/strong&gt; It has a huge data asset, licensing revenue and lawyers. Layers 1, 4 and 6 are cheap for anyone; layers 2 and 3 (litigation, a paid API with licensing deals) need money and a content library worth paying for.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Blocking agents blocks assistants.&lt;/strong&gt; Your own users now ask AI tools to read pages for them. A site that blocks all agents also blocks those users; decide per audience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Search visibility is a real cost.&lt;/strong&gt; Reddit accepted losing Bing and DuckDuckGo because it sells access. A site that depends on search traffic should not copy that.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Facts here come from press and a lawsuit.&lt;/strong&gt; Allegations are marked as allegations, and Reddit has not published its detection internals.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Bottom Line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reddit did not find a trick. It stacked six ordinary layers, from a text file to a lawsuit, and attached a price to the way in. For most sites, the practical version is three steps: name the AI crawlers in robots.txt, put rate limiting or a bot filter in front of the site, and plant a canary so you can tell if it matters.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Resources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;TechCrunch, Reddit's robots.txt and bot changes (June 2024): &lt;a href="https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/reddits-upcoming-changes-attempt-to-safeguard-the-platform-against-ai-crawlers" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://techcrunch.com/2024/06/25/reddits-upcoming-changes-attempt-to-safeguard-the-platform-against-ai-crawlers&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Register: &lt;a href="https://www.theregister.com/2024/06/27/reddit_ai_info" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.theregister.com/2024/06/27/reddit_ai_info&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;9to5Mac, Reddit blocking search engines (July 2024): &lt;a href="https://9to5mac.com/2024/07/25/reddit-blocking-search-engines/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://9to5mac.com/2024/07/25/reddit-blocking-search-engines/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Search Engine Land, Reddit v. Perplexity/SerpApi: &lt;a href="https://searchengineland.com/reddit-sues-perplexity-serpapi-scraping-google-463681" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://searchengineland.com/reddit-sues-perplexity-serpapi-scraping-google-463681&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;PPC Land, summary of the complaint: &lt;a href="https://ppc.land/reddit-sues-data-scrapers-and-perplexity-over-unauthorized-content-access/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://ppc.land/reddit-sues-data-scrapers-and-perplexity-over-unauthorized-content-access/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;PPC Land, Cloudflare on stealth crawlers: &lt;a href="https://ppc.land/perplexity-denies-training-ai-models-as-cloudflare-documents-stealth-crawlers/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://ppc.land/perplexity-denies-training-ai-models-as-cloudflare-documents-stealth-crawlers/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;SiliconANGLE, Wayback Machine restriction: &lt;a href="https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/11/reddit-says-blocking-internet-archive-stop-sneaky-ai-scrapers-accessing-content/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://siliconangle.com/2025/08/11/reddit-says-blocking-internet-archive-stop-sneaky-ai-scrapers-accessing-content/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;MacRumors, Apollo and API pricing: &lt;a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2023/05/31/reddit-api-changes-pricing-apollo/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.macrumors.com/2023/05/31/reddit-api-changes-pricing-apollo/&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Engadget, RSL standard: &lt;a href="https://www.engadget.com/ai/reddit-yahoo-medium-and-more-are-adopting-a-new-licensing-standard-to-get-compensated-for-ai-scraping-180946671.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.engadget.com/ai/reddit-yahoo-medium-and-more-are-adopting-a-new-licensing-standard-to-get-compensated-for-ai-scraping-180946671.html&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) lets you rent servers, storage and networks over the internet and pay only for what you use, instead of buying hardware. In this article we unpack what IaaS is, how it compares to PaaS and SaaS, and then use Amazon EC2 on AWS as a hands-on case study: you will launch a real web server in the cloud in a few minutes.&lt;br&gt;
If you have ever waited weeks for a physical server, or paid for one that sat idle most of the month, IaaS is the fix.&lt;br&gt;
What is IaaS?&lt;br&gt;
IaaS is the cloud service model where a provider runs the physical data centre, hardware and virtualization, and you get on-demand virtual machines, disks and networks to build on. You still manage the operating system, runtime, applications and data.&lt;br&gt;
The easiest way to see where IaaS fits is to compare who manages what in each model:&lt;br&gt;
Layer&lt;br&gt;
On-premises&lt;br&gt;
IaaS&lt;br&gt;
PaaS&lt;br&gt;
SaaS&lt;br&gt;
Applications&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Data&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Runtime and middleware&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Operating system&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Virtualization&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Servers, storage, networking&lt;br&gt;
You&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Provider&lt;br&gt;
Example&lt;br&gt;
Your own server room&lt;br&gt;
Amazon EC2, Azure VMs, Google Compute Engine&lt;br&gt;
Heroku, Google App Engine&lt;br&gt;
Gmail, Slack&lt;br&gt;
In short: IaaS gives you the most control of the three cloud models, and with it the most responsibility.&lt;br&gt;
The building blocks of IaaS&lt;br&gt;
Every IaaS platform, whatever it calls them, offers the same core pieces:&lt;br&gt;
• Compute: virtual machines you size by CPU and memory, started and stopped on demand.&lt;br&gt;
• Storage: block storage (virtual disks attached to a VM), object storage (files and backups) and file storage (shared drives).&lt;br&gt;
• Networking: private virtual networks, subnets, public IPs, firewalls and load balancers.&lt;br&gt;
• Identity and access: users, roles and keys that control who can create or touch resources.&lt;br&gt;
• Monitoring and automation: metrics, logs, alerts and APIs so infrastructure can be scripted instead of clicked.&lt;br&gt;
Because everything is exposed through an API, you can treat infrastructure as code: describe servers in a file and recreate them identically at any time.&lt;br&gt;
Case study: Amazon EC2&lt;br&gt;
Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2), launched by AWS in 2006, is one of the oldest and most widely used IaaS offerings. It runs workloads ranging from student side projects to large streaming services such as Netflix, which moved its infrastructure onto AWS over several years.&lt;br&gt;
Here is how the generic IaaS building blocks map to EC2 and its neighbouring AWS services:&lt;br&gt;
IaaS building block&lt;br&gt;
AWS service or feature&lt;br&gt;
What it does&lt;br&gt;
Virtual machine&lt;br&gt;
EC2 instance&lt;br&gt;
A server you choose by instance type (CPU, memory, network)&lt;br&gt;
Machine image&lt;br&gt;
Amazon Machine Image (AMI)&lt;br&gt;
Template with the OS and software to boot from&lt;br&gt;
Block storage&lt;br&gt;
Amazon EBS volume&lt;br&gt;
A persistent virtual disk attached to an instance&lt;br&gt;
Object storage&lt;br&gt;
Amazon S3&lt;br&gt;
Stores files, backups and static assets&lt;br&gt;
Private network&lt;br&gt;
Amazon VPC and subnets&lt;br&gt;
Your isolated network in the AWS cloud&lt;br&gt;
Firewall&lt;br&gt;
Security groups&lt;br&gt;
Allow or deny traffic by port and source&lt;br&gt;
Access control&lt;br&gt;
IAM users and roles; SSH key pairs&lt;br&gt;
Who can manage resources and log in to servers&lt;br&gt;
Scaling&lt;br&gt;
Auto Scaling groups and Elastic Load Balancing&lt;br&gt;
Add or remove instances with demand and spread traffic&lt;br&gt;
Monitoring&lt;br&gt;
Amazon CloudWatch&lt;br&gt;
Metrics, logs and alarms&lt;br&gt;
EC2 instance types are grouped into families by purpose: general purpose (the T and M families), compute optimized (C), memory optimized (R and X), storage optimized (I and D) and accelerated computing with GPUs (P and G). Picking the right family is the first real cost and performance decision you make.&lt;br&gt;
Hands-on: launch a web server on EC2&lt;br&gt;
You will launch a small Linux instance that serves a web page with Nginx. You need an AWS account; new accounts get Free Tier benefits, so check the current terms on the AWS Free Tier page before you start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Sign in to the AWS Management Console, pick a region close to your users, and open EC2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Click Launch instance and give it a name, for example my-first-iaas-server.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Under Application and OS Images, choose Amazon Linux 2023.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Under Instance type, choose a small general-purpose type such as t3.micro.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Under Key pair, create a new key pair and download the .pem file. You cannot download it again.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Under Network settings, allow SSH from My IP only, and allow HTTP from anywhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Expand Advanced details and paste this into User data, so the server configures itself on first boot:
#!/bin/bash
dnf update -y
dnf install -y nginx
echo "&lt;h1&gt;Hello from IaaS on Amazon EC2&lt;/h1&gt;" &amp;gt; /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html
systemctl enable --now nginx&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Click Launch instance, wait until the state is Running, then copy its Public IPv4 address.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Open http:// in a browser. You should see your Hello page.
To log in to the server itself, use the key pair you downloaded:
chmod 400 my-key.pem
ssh -i my-key.pem ec2-user@
Prefer the terminal? With the AWS CLI configured, the same launch is one command (replace the placeholder IDs with your own):
aws ec2 run-instances \
--image-id  \
--instance-type t3.micro \
--key-name my-key \
--security-group-ids  \
--user-data file://setup.sh \
--tag-specifications 'ResourceType=instance,Tags=[{Key=Name,Value=my-first-iaas-server}]'
Clean up when you are done: select the instance and choose Instance state → Terminate so you are not billed for a server you forgot about.
Notice what just happened: no hardware purchase, no cabling, no waiting. That is the core promise of IaaS.
Pricing, benefits and challenges
EC2 bills you only for the capacity you run, and offers several pricing models depending on how predictable your workload is:
Pricing model
Best for
Trade-off
On-Demand
New projects, unpredictable traffic
Highest per-hour price, no commitment
Savings Plans and Reserved Instances
Steady workloads running for 1 or 3 years
Big discount in exchange for a commitment
Spot Instances
Batch jobs, CI builds, fault-tolerant work
Steepest discount, but AWS can reclaim the instance at short notice
Dedicated Hosts
Licensing or compliance needs
Physical server reserved for you, at a premium
Exact prices vary by region and instance type, so use the AWS Pricing Calculator before committing.
Benefits of IaaS, as seen with EC2:
• Speed: a server is ready in minutes, not weeks.
• Elasticity: scale out for a traffic spike, scale back in when it ends.
• No upfront capital cost: hardware becomes an operating expense.
• Global reach: deploy close to users in AWS regions around the world.
• Control: full choice of OS, software and network layout.
Challenges to plan for:
• Shared responsibility: AWS secures the data centre; you must patch the OS, lock down security groups and protect keys.
• Cost surprises: forgotten instances, unattached volumes and data transfer out can grow the bill quietly. Set billing alerts.
• Skills needed: networking, Linux administration and security are now your job.
• Vendor lock-in: heavy use of provider-specific services makes moving clouds harder.
When to choose IaaS
Choose IaaS when you need control that PaaS and SaaS do not give you, for example:
• Migrating existing servers to the cloud with minimal changes ("lift and shift").
• Running custom software, specific OS versions or unusual networking setups.
• Handling spiky or seasonal traffic you cannot predict.
• Spinning up short-lived environments for testing, training or data processing.
If you just want to deploy code without managing servers, a PaaS will save you time. If you just need the finished software, buy SaaS.
Conclusion
IaaS turns physical infrastructure into something you can create, resize and delete with a click or a line of code. Amazon EC2 shows the model clearly: you pick an image and an instance type, attach storage, open the right ports, and you have a server running in minutes, billed only while it runs. The trade-off is that the OS, security and cost discipline are yours to own.
Try the hands-on steps above, then terminate the instance. Next, explore Auto Scaling groups and a load balancer to see the elastic side of IaaS in action.
Have you used EC2 or another IaaS platform such as Azure or Google Cloud? Share your experience in the comments.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you watch competitor prices, you do not want 200 rows every morning. You want the 3 rows where something moved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yesterday I wrote about &lt;a href="https://dev.to/martinpython/compare-one-products-price-across-online-stores-with-python-real-output-3dd5"&gt;comparing one product's price across online stores&lt;/a&gt;. At the end I showed a small manual diff and said a built-in change mode was next. It is live now, so this post shows how to use it: a daily run that returns only offers whose price or stock changed, with the old price, the new price and the change in percent. Real output from today (2026-11-11) included, plus the limits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why the usual approach hurts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A home-made price watch usually grows like this:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Scrape every product page on a schedule.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Save all rows somewhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Load yesterday's rows, match them by URL, compare price and stock.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Decide what counts as a change (currency rounding, sale price, "out of stock").&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Store today's rows as the new baseline, without losing them when a run fails halfway.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pay for storing and processing hundreds of rows that did not change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Steps 3 to 5 are the boring part, and they are where these scripts break: one failed run overwrites the baseline and the next day every row looks "new".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the change mode does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built the Cheapest Price Finder: you give it product page URLs from any stores, it reads the real price from each page's structured data (JSON-LD offers, Shopify product data, meta price tags), converts everything to one currency with daily ECB rates and sorts the offers from cheapest to most expensive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two inputs turn it into a price watch:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;onlyChanged: true&lt;/code&gt; saves only offers whose price or stock changed since the last run, plus pages it has never seen. Unchanged offers are not saved and not billed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;monitorId&lt;/code&gt; (optional) names the watch, e.g. &lt;code&gt;aeropress-eu&lt;/code&gt;. Runs with the same &lt;code&gt;monitorId&lt;/code&gt; share the remembered prices even when you add or remove URLs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every returned row gets &lt;code&gt;changeType&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;new&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;priceDrop&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;priceIncrease&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;availability&lt;/code&gt;), &lt;code&gt;previousPrice&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;priceChange&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;priceChangePercent&lt;/code&gt;. These are in the shop's own currency (the &lt;code&gt;price&lt;/code&gt; field), so a moving exchange rate never looks like a price change. The state lives in your own Apify account, so your watch is yours alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: get a token
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create a free Apify account and copy your token from Console (Settings, API and Integrations). Put it in an environment variable:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;APIFY_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;your_token_here
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: the first run with curl
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -X POST &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://api.apify.com/v2/acts/martin10291~cheapest-price-finder/run-sync-get-dataset-items?token=$APIFY_TOKEN"&lt;/span&gt; \
-H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Content-Type: application/json'&lt;/span&gt; \
-d &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"productUrls": [
"https://www.vervecoffee.com/products/aeropress-coffee-brewer",
"https://www.intelligentsia.com/products/aeropress",
"https://www.espressoparts.com/products/aeropress-coffee-maker",
"https://www.stumptowncoffee.com/products/aeropress"],
"convertTo": "EUR",
"onlyChanged": true,
"monitorId": "aeropress-eu"}'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The first run has nothing to compare with, so every offer comes back as &lt;code&gt;new&lt;/code&gt;. That is your baseline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: the same thing in Python
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="no"&gt;TOKEN&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"APIFY_TOKEN"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# your own token&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://api.apify.com/v2/acts/martin10291~cheapest-price-finder/"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"run-sync-get-dataset-items"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="no"&gt;URLS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://www.vervecoffee.com/products/aeropress-coffee-brewer"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://www.intelligentsia.com/products/aeropress"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://www.espressoparts.com/products/aeropress-coffee-maker"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://www.stumptowncoffee.com/products/aeropress"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;price_changes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;urls&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;watch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"aeropress-eu"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;currency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"EUR"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"token"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;180&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"productUrls"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;urls&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"convertTo"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;currency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"onlyChanged"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"monitorId"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;watch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raise_for_status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;price_changes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;URLS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;o&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"changeType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"new"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"NEW   {o['store']:22} {o['priceConverted']:&amp;gt;7} EUR  {o['availability']}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"{o['changeType']:13} {o['store']:22} "&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"{o['previousPrice']} -&amp;gt; {o['price']} {o['currency']} "&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"({o['priceChangePercent']:+}%)  {o['availability']}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Real output from today
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I ran the same input twice, a few minutes apart, with &lt;code&gt;monitorId&lt;/code&gt; set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Run 1&lt;/strong&gt; (9.5 s, 4 rows):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;NEW   vervecoffee.com          35.63 EUR  InStock
NEW   intelligentsia.com       35.67 EUR  OutOfStock
NEW   espressoparts.com        40.08 EUR  InStock
NEW   stumptowncoffee.com      41.02 EUR  InStock
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One row as JSON (trimmed):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"rankByPrice"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"isCheapest"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"store"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"vervecoffee.com"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"price"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;39.95&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"currency"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"USD"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceConverted"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;35.63&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"convertedCurrency"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"EUR"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"availability"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"InStock"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"changeType"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"new"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"previousPrice"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceChange"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceChangePercent"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"url"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://www.vervecoffee.com/products/aeropress-coffee-brewer"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Run 2&lt;/strong&gt; (6.1 s, 0 rows). No price moved in those minutes, so the dataset is empty and the run was charged for 0 results. The run's &lt;code&gt;RUN_SUMMARY&lt;/code&gt; record tells you it still checked everything:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"pagesChecked"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"offers"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"failed"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"lastError"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"monitor"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"monitorId"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"devto-aeropress-1111"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"firstRun"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"onlyChanged"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"changes"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"new"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceDrop"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priceIncrease"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"availability"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"unchanged"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"rowsSaved"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I did not invent a price drop for this post. When one of these shops changes a price, the row comes back with &lt;code&gt;changeType: "priceDrop"&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;"priceIncrease"&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;previousPrice&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;priceChange&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;priceChangePercent&lt;/code&gt; filled. An "in stock" to "out of stock" switch comes back as &lt;code&gt;availability&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An empty dataset on a quiet day is the point: your alert code runs only on real changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: run it every day
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The simplest way is an Apify schedule:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open the &lt;a href="https://apify.com/martin10291/cheapest-price-finder" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;actor page&lt;/a&gt;, paste your URLs, tick "Only changed offers" and type a Monitor ID.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Save it as a Task.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add a schedule, e.g. every day at 07:00.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Optional: add a webhook on "run succeeded" that calls your own endpoint, or connect the dataset to Google Sheets, Slack or email with Apify integrations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you prefer your own cron, call &lt;code&gt;price_changes()&lt;/code&gt; from a script and send the rows wherever you like:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;0 7 * * * /usr/bin/python3 /home/me/price_changes.py &amp;gt;&amp;gt; /home/me/prices.log 2&amp;gt;&amp;amp;1
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add or remove URLs at any time. With the same &lt;code&gt;monitorId&lt;/code&gt;, old URLs keep their remembered prices and new URLs come back as &lt;code&gt;new&lt;/code&gt; once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The API.market option
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same tool is on &lt;a href="https://api.market/store/martindata/price-comparison-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;API.market&lt;/a&gt; with a free plan, if you want one API key and simple HTTP:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;curl -X POST &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://prod.api.market/api/v1/martindata/price-comparison-api/m/cheapest-price-finder'&lt;/span&gt; \
-H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'x-api-market-key: YOUR_API_MARKET_KEY'&lt;/span&gt; \
-H &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Content-Type: application/json'&lt;/span&gt; \
-d &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'{"productUrls": ["https://www.espressoparts.com/products/aeropress-coffee-maker"],
"convertTo": "EUR"}'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;One honest difference: on API.market the change mode is switched off. The remembered prices would live in my account, shared by every caller with the same URLs, and one customer's run would "use up" another customer's changes. So there you get the full sorted offers every time and keep the diff yourself (the 10-line diff from &lt;a href="https://dev.to/martinpython/compare-one-products-price-across-online-stores-with-python-real-output-3dd5"&gt;the first post&lt;/a&gt; works). For a built-in change feed, use the Apify actor with your own token.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Honest limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Big marketplaces often block automated requests.&lt;/strong&gt; Amazon, Walmart, Target, Best Buy and Newegg frequently answer with a bot-check page. The tool does not bypass those; it reports them in &lt;code&gt;RUN_SUMMARY.failed&lt;/code&gt; and does not charge for them. Brand stores and independent shops (Shopify, WooCommerce, Magento, BigCommerce) work best.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The first run is never empty&lt;/strong&gt;: every page is &lt;code&gt;new&lt;/code&gt; once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No shipping costs or coupon codes.&lt;/strong&gt; It compares the listed product price.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It does not match products for you.&lt;/strong&gt; You choose which URLs are the same item; &lt;code&gt;gtin&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sku&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;brand&lt;/code&gt; help you check.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pages that build the price only with JavaScript&lt;/strong&gt; and publish no product data return "no price found".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Price
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Apify it costs $3.00 per 1,000 saved results (lower with Apify's Bronze, Silver and Gold plans). With &lt;code&gt;onlyChanged&lt;/code&gt;, unchanged offers are not saved, so a quiet day costs nothing for results. My two runs above together cost under one tenth of a cent of platform usage. Apify's free plan includes a small monthly credit, enough to try this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On API.market there is a free plan to test, then the paid plans shown on the &lt;a href="https://api.market/store/martindata/price-comparison-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;listing&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Wrap-up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built this because every price script I wrote ended up as the same "compare with yesterday" code, and it was the part that broke. Now the tool keeps yesterday for you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you watch prices for a shop, a reseller business or a deal newsletter, try it on &lt;a href="https://apify.com/martin10291/cheapest-price-finder" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Apify&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://api.market/store/martindata/price-comparison-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;API.market&lt;/a&gt;. Which shop would you add to the watch first? If it returns "no price found", post the URL in the comments and I will look at it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every time I attend a conference like AI Engineer Code Summit, I wish I had a space to try what I learned. Today’s hackathon is meant to be that space for each of you. It’s an opportunity to experiment and play with everything you’ve absorbed. Whether you attended in person or watched the livestreams, we all spent the last three days hearing from the industry’s best AI practitioners and engineers. Now you get to put those ideas to work. I’m jealous. If I weren’t helping run the hackathon, I would be where you are. Use the time to build something awesome.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;AI Engineer Code Summit had three core themes this year: building with System One models, fighting slop in software factories, and rewriting critical apps. TypeSafe’s Jev catapulted System One (decision models) into the limelight. If you’re feeling out of the loop, Jev launched only two months ago, on September 15th, and with how fast our industry moves, that may already feel like years ago. Unlike typical chat or reasoning LLMs, these models skip chain-of-thought generation to return fast, calibrated, strictly typed decisions. Other labs have quickly followed TypeSafe’s success with decision models of their own. If you haven’t used these yet, they’re my personal recommendation for your hack. There are so many cool, unexplored use cases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Beyond System One models, the conference aimed to reduce slop in software factories. Dark and light software factories are popular terms to describe how humans interact with these automated pipelines. In a dark factory, the machine is designed to run itself: you minimally review intermediate code because you front-load inputs, like specs and repo rules, and automate output verification. Light factories keep humans more regularly in the loop. Lauren “poteto” Tan from SpaceXSI had a fantastic keynote on Wednesday about setting your codebase up for success. Lauren famously ships thousands of PRs a month with her pstack system. Even with limited time at the hackathon, setting up your repo, agent skills, and codebase upfront pays dividends over jumping straight into development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Initially, I feared rewriting critical apps was the least relevant theme for today’s hackathon, but thinking about it more, I disagree. We have superpowers right now. We can build performant apps from the get-go. Last week, Discord announced a native rewrite of their application in Rust. Before the current AI cycle, I would never have reached for Rust to build a hackathon project. Today, agents are quite capable of writing code in any language. Lower-level, efficient languages are no longer a bottleneck to shipping fast, making today a great opportunity to experiment with an unfamiliar language or framework.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I hope you use today to experiment and apply new knowledge from AI Engineer Code Summit. Hearing from our industry’s best developers makes a trip to any AI Engineer conference worthwhile, though I’ve always struggled with the follow-up. I leave a conference with 100 great ideas and no time to make them. Take one of yours and make something today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Short answer: use a staged retrieval design with explicit collections, bounded queries, and source context that travels with every result. For a healthcare appointment assistant, the answer is not finished when a model produces fluent text. A reviewer needs to see which scheduling policy, clinic record, or insurance note grounded it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I run a one-person SaaS, so my decision rule is revenue per hour: ship weekly, and outsource the undifferentiated plumbing. Citation tracing is worth designing before the first endpoint call because retrofitting provenance after launch means re-indexing content and rewriting answer code under pressure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For this workflow, Infrai is worth testing early when the team wants to inspect a capability's schema and runnable examples before committing to another SDK. Its public discovery surface can shorten the path from “we need bounded vector retrieval” to a reviewed adapter, while the citation contract remains an application responsibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should retrieval architecture trace citations in a healthcare appointment assistant?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start by mapping each user-visible answer to a retrieval contract. “Can I book a Saturday dermatology visit?” needs a bounded set of records, a freshness expectation, and a citation shape. The contract can be as small as &lt;code&gt;{sourceId, sourceUrl, excerpt, retrievedAt}&lt;/code&gt;. The exact field names are yours; the invariant is that generation cannot discard the evidence. In practice, I would make the adapter reject a result whose &lt;code&gt;sourceId&lt;/code&gt; is missing, preserve the source's revision or effective date when one exists, and carry the same identifier into the answer log. Imagine two records that both say a clinic accepts new patients: one is an old onboarding page and one is a policy revision from this week. A similarity score alone can prefer the older wording. A contract with freshness metadata lets the ranker or a post-filter choose the current record, and it gives a reviewer a concrete trail instead of a vague “the model was confident.” This is extra typing up front. It is cheaper than explaining an uncited booking decision later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep collections explicit. A &lt;code&gt;clinic-policies&lt;/code&gt; collection should not silently absorb provider bios or patient-facing FAQs. Separate collections make deletion and re-indexing deliberate, and they give the review screen a useful scope label. Query with a small limit and a timeout. A slow source should not hold the appointment flow hostage.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the smallest TypeScript client I would put behind that contract. Discovery is public, so the process can inspect the platform schema before wiring a capability. The query body is supplied by your adapter, which keeps domain fields out of infrastructure code.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://api.infrai.cc/v1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CitationContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;sourceId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;sourceUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;excerpt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;retrievedAt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;postVectorQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timeoutMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"INFRAI_API_KEY is required"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AbortController&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timeoutMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${baseUrl}/vector/query`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Bearer ${key}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"retry-after"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;wait&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;postVectorQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;timeoutMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4800&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Vector query failed (${response.status}): ${await response.text()}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;CitationContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;clearTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The retry is bounded, and the caller still owns a hard deadline. For production, add a retry budget and make the adapter reject results without a source identifier. A citation that cannot be opened or matched to an internal document is a failed retrieval, not a cosmetic omission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A four-step build log
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Define the contract.&lt;/strong&gt; Write the answer fields and citation fields next to each other. Store the source URL or document identifier at ingestion time, not after generation. This is where you decide that “opening hours” must cite a policy revision, while “next available slot” must cite the scheduling record returned for that request.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Create and populate one collection per trust boundary.&lt;/strong&gt; Use the collection-create and upsert capabilities exposed by the vector surface, then record the collection name with the document manifest. Keep the manifest authoritative. When a clinic edits a policy, re-index the changed record deliberately; when a record is deleted, remove it from the collection instead of leaving a stale embedding that can still be retrieved.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3. Query with limits.&lt;/strong&gt; Apply a result limit, metadata filters that your adapter owns, and a timeout appropriate for an interactive appointment screen. Merge results from multiple sources only after normalizing their citation fields. Do not let a high-scoring but expired policy outrank a current one merely because its wording is similar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;4. Generate and audit.&lt;/strong&gt; Pass retrieved excerpts plus their identifiers to the model. Persist the request id and the citations shown to the user. A reviewer should be able to replay the retrieval contract and see why a slot was offered. If the evidence is thin, return a clarification request instead of inventing an appointment detail.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last branch matters. “I don't have a current source for that slot” is a useful product behavior; an uncited guess is a clinical liability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No citation, no answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Where do the main options differ?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The storage engine is only part of the integration cost. I compare the credential model, SDK surface, and first useful result because those determine how many hours remain for product work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Integration shape&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Citation-tracing fit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pinecone&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Managed vector database with focused APIs and SDKs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Metadata can carry document IDs and URLs; you assemble the audit path&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Choose it when vector search is the product and you want a specialist control plane&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Weaviate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vector database with a broad schema and module ecosystem&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Objects can retain properties for provenance and filtering&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Choose it when you need its schema-first modules or self-hosting options&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Elasticsearch&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Search platform combining lexical and vector retrieval&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Strong document-level metadata and mature filtering&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Choose it when your team already operates Elastic and needs hybrid search&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Infrai&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One REST API with public discovery and runnable examples&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A self-describing discovery surface helps wire a capability while keeping your citation contract in application code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Choose a specialist when you need deep, engine-specific tuning or a search cluster you already run&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai's practical advantage here is integration friction: discovery returns a capability's request and response schemas plus runnable examples, so wiring a new backend action is reading one endpoint rather than learning another SDK. The same key and plain HTTP surface can cover adjacent backend work, which removes credential and dependency juggling from a small team. It does not remove the need to design provenance, retention, or clinical review rules yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What would I change at scale?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At low volume, a single worker and a manifest table are enough. As sources multiply, I would split ingestion, embedding, and answer serving into queues, then attach a revision number to every document. Re-indexing becomes an observable job with a clear before-and-after count. Deletion becomes a tested event, not a manual console action.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would also keep retrieval evaluation separate from model evaluation. Track citation recall, stale-document rate, and the percentage of answers that correctly abstain. Your mileage may vary because clinic data quality and policy cadence dominate those numbers; the architecture should make the uncertainty visible instead of hiding it behind a confidence score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The catch is operational focus. Infrai is a good fit when a solo team values a self-describing REST surface and one integration boundary for several capabilities. It is not suitable when your compliance program requires a particular regional search deployment, or when a dedicated engine's analyzers and ranking controls are non-negotiable. Stick with Pinecone, Weaviate, or Elasticsearch in those cases, and keep the same citation contract.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a concrete starting point, read the public &lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Infrai documentation&lt;/a&gt; and verify the discovered schema before implementing your adapter. Then test the contract with real, de-identified appointment questions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Further reading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.infrai.cc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.infrai.cc&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.pinecone.io/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://docs.pinecone.io/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://weaviate.io/developers/weaviate" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://weaviate.io/developers/weaviate&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/dense-vector.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/elasticsearch/reference/current/dense-vector.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every growing codebase carries debt. The question is not whether you have it, but whether it is priced, prioritized, and paid down deliberately. Teams that treat debt as a moral failure tend to oscillate between denial and expensive rescue projects. Teams that treat it as a portfolio make calmer, faster decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Price the debt before you rank it&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most debt backlogs fail because every item is described in engineering language: "upgrade the ORM," "split the monolith," "remove the circular dependency." Executives cannot compare those items to a revenue feature. Translate each item into one of three currencies: &lt;strong&gt;delivery drag&lt;/strong&gt; (weeks lost per quarter), &lt;strong&gt;risk exposure&lt;/strong&gt; (incident likelihood and blast radius), or &lt;strong&gt;cost of operation&lt;/strong&gt; (infrastructure and support hours).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once debt is priced, it belongs in the same portfolio review as product work. You are no longer asking for "engineering time." You are choosing between two investments with different return profiles.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;The 20% capacity contract&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Reserve a standing slice of every sprint for engineering health, and protect it like a customer commitment. The exact number matters less than the contract. Twenty percent is enough to make progress without starving the roadmap; ten percent is enough to stop the bleeding; zero percent is a decision to borrow at a higher interest rate later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;70% product:&lt;/strong&gt; committed roadmap work with a named customer outcome.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;20% engineering health:&lt;/strong&gt; debt reduction, reliability, and developer experience.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;10% discovery:&lt;/strong&gt; spikes and prototypes with an explicit expiry date.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The split is a planning heuristic, not a law. During a launch window you may temporarily shift the mix — but write down when it returns to normal. Unwritten exceptions become the new baseline.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;Make the cost of delay visible&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use cycle-time data to show what debt actually costs. If the checkout team's lead time has grown from four days to eleven over two quarters, that is a business metric, not a code-quality complaint. Pair it with incident data and support volume. Leaders do not need to understand your architecture to understand a trend line that is moving in the wrong direction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;Debt is not the enemy. Unpriced debt is. Once the cost is visible, the conversation shifts from principle to prioritization.&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;A quarterly review that actually works&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run a 45-minute debt review every quarter with engineering and product leadership in the room. Keep it ruthlessly short:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review the three largest debt items and their business impact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Confirm what was retired since the last review — celebrate the deletions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Approve the next quarter's funded debt work, with an owner and a deadline.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Record any accepted risk explicitly, including who owns it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The output is a one-page decision record, not a 40-slide deck. The goal is a shared memory of what you chose to carry and why. That memory is what keeps the same debate from reopening every sprint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;What good looks like&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Healthy teams can name their top three debt risks, show the delivery drag each one creates, and point to funded work in progress. They delete more than they add. And when they choose to carry debt for a strategic reason, they do it with their eyes open — not by accident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every freelancer has experienced the 'airplane mode panic.' You’re sitting in a coffee shop with spotty Wi-Fi, the client is ready to sign, and your SaaS-based invoicing tool is stuck on a spinning loading wheel, failing to authenticate against a remote server. It’s a failure of architectural philosophy: why should your ability to generate a PDF depend on a server halfway across the world?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When we started building &lt;a href="https://vanzalabs.tech/invoices" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Vanza Invoices&lt;/a&gt;, we decided to stop treating the browser and mobile device as thin clients. We inverted the model. The device is the source of truth; the cloud is optional, or completely irrelevant.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Death of the 'Sync-Everything' Middleware
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most modern apps rely on a 'sync-everything' middleware that pipes data to a central database before reflecting it back to the UI. This introduces latency, subscription overhead, and catastrophic failure points when the connection drops. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We moved to a local-first storage architecture utilizing IndexedDB, wrapped in a reactive persistence layer. By treating the local storage as the primary database, the app achieves sub-10ms read/write operations. When a user creates an invoice, the data lives exactly where it was typed—on the device. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the stack logic we implemented:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Persistence Layer:&lt;/strong&gt; We leverage Dexie.js for a robust IndexedDB wrapper, allowing for complex queries without hitting a network request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;State Synchronization:&lt;/strong&gt; Instead of REST/GraphQL calls, the UI binds directly to the local database change listeners. If an invoice is updated, the view refreshes automatically.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Asset Generation:&lt;/strong&gt; PDFs are generated on-device using a worker thread to ensure the main UI thread remains buttery smooth during signature rendering and complex document assembly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Solving the 'Zero-Middleman' PDF Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Generating a professional invoice typically happens on the server. You send JSON to an API, a server-side process (like Puppeteer or a specialized library) renders the PDF, and it’s piped back to you. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At Vanza, we stripped the middleman. By bringing the document engine to the client, we eliminated the need for a monthly SaaS subscription. There is no server cost to pass on to the user. We utilized specialized PDF generation primitives that execute natively in the browser/Capacitor environment. This allows for:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Privacy by Default:&lt;/strong&gt; Your financial data never leaves your device unless you explicitly export it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Instant Previews:&lt;/strong&gt; Since we aren't waiting for a server round-trip, the document updates in real-time as you type.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Offline Signature Capture:&lt;/strong&gt; Using a canvas-based touch handler, we capture high-fidelity vector signatures that are embedded directly into the final PDF buffer via the browser’s internal file system API.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Capacitor Advantage
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We chose React and Capacitor to bridge the gap between web speed and native reliability. By shipping the application as a bundled binary, we bypass the browser's aggressive cache-clearing policies. Your invoices are stored in the app’s private container on your OS, effectively making them as durable as any native file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For developers considering this route, the biggest hurdle isn't writing the code—it's managing the data schema migrations locally. Since you don't have a backend to migrate, your local database schema must be versioned within the app binary itself. We rely on a strict migration strategy triggered on initial app startup, ensuring that as we add new invoice fields, older data structures are upgraded seamlessly without loss.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why We Built This
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built &lt;a href="https://vanzalabs.tech/invoices" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Vanza Invoices&lt;/a&gt; because the freelance economy is tired of paying rent for the privilege of creating a simple invoice. By leveraging local-first storage and offloading the computation to the hardware you already own, we’ve created a tool that lasts as long as your phone does, with zero dependency on our servers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are a developer or a freelancer looking for a tool that respects your privacy and works without a signal, check out our implementation on the &lt;a href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=app.vanza.docs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Google Play Store&lt;/a&gt;. It is a testament to what is possible when you stop building for the cloud and start building for the machine in your pocket.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hey everyone 👋&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My portfolio has five themes: Classic, Terminal, Notebook, Midnight and Panorama. A dropdown would have been the easy way to switch between them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted switching themes to feel like &lt;strong&gt;plugging a cable into a screen&lt;/strong&gt;. So I built a Theme Studio.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmg1fa2h59nfwhc3j12dz.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmg1fa2h59nfwhc3j12dz.gif" alt="Wires connection" width="400" height="244"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What you can do 🎮
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pick a theme&lt;/strong&gt; from the row of cards, and it slides in under the display&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Watch the display&lt;/strong&gt;: it plays that theme's real page, live, and you can scroll it&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Drag the display or a card around&lt;/strong&gt;, and the wires follow on a spring&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hit Open&lt;/strong&gt;: light races up the wires, a progress bar fills on the screen, and the theme opens&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Press Esc mid-send&lt;/strong&gt; to pull the plug&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The wires 🔌
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The connection isn't one line. It's a &lt;strong&gt;bundle of hairline strands&lt;/strong&gt;, gold through orange, red and magenta to violet, pinched in the middle like a cable tie.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Every strand is its own curve.&lt;/strong&gt; Each one is a cubic bezier from a point on the card's edge to a point on the display's edge, so the bundle fans out at both ends.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Light runs down it.&lt;/strong&gt; Bands of brighter colour travel along the strands, and in dark mode they're blended additively so overlapping strands glow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It has weight.&lt;/strong&gt; Drag a node and the bundle's middle trails behind on a spring, then settles.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sending is a volley.&lt;/strong&gt; When you open a theme, a burst of bands and short sparks race up the strands into the display.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's all one canvas over the stage, redrawn every frame.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The progress bar 🟩🟦🟪
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While the light travels, the display's screen goes dark and a pixel progress bar fills on it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F37pscsblckdwbg23pgeg.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F37pscsblckdwbg23pgeg.gif" alt="Progress bars" width="400" height="222"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One strict grid.&lt;/strong&gt; Every cell sits on a 6px grid: lit cells are 4px squares (or dots), unlit ones are tiny specks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Checkers, not noise.&lt;/strong&gt; Which cells light up is an ordered dither of slowly drifting noise, so they gather into checker and diamond patterns that keep shifting.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A white-hot edge.&lt;/strong&gt; The leading edge burns white with a soft bloom, and a few cells flare and go out as it fills.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Each theme gets its own bar.&lt;/strong&gt; The wire's colours for Classic, GitHub greens for Terminal, blue dots for Panorama, and one colour per section for Midnight and Notebook.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It doesn't lie.&lt;/strong&gt; The bar holds at 92% until the theme's page has actually loaded.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Under the bar, the site's sections (&lt;strong&gt;Intro, Work, Articles, Blocks&lt;/strong&gt;) fill in turn as the bar passes through them, each with its own percentage. Point at one and its stretch of the bar lights up while the rest dims.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When it hits 100%, the screen grows out of the display and dives into the page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Bonus: you're not alone 👀
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If someone else is on the page, you'll see their cursor and their wire too. That runs through a small Cloudflare Durable Object.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it 🌐
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;a href="https://mahmad.me/theme-studio" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;mahmad.me/theme-studio&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plug in Terminal and watch the greens fill up, then try Panorama.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Skip a headless CMS if you write the posts yourself: use Markdown. If someone else writes them, they need an editor, and a headless CMS still leaves you building the blog. That's what DropInBlog is for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask how to add a blog to an Astro or Next.js site, and you get a list. Sanity, Payload, Strapi, Contentful, Directus. Pick one, wire up the API, build some templates, and ship it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick disclosure: I founded &lt;a href="https://dropinblog.com/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=headless-cms-blog" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DropInBlog&lt;/a&gt; and have been building blog software since 2015. I also use it on my own Astro site, so I'm biased, and you should weigh everything below accordingly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of you should skip us too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Who's going to write the posts?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I started building websites at 19, back when "layout" meant Dreamweaver and a lot of nested tables. That turned into a web agency, and our clients had custom-built sites. Sooner or later, every one of them asked for a blog.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The standard fix was WordPress on a subdomain with a custom theme built to match. It worked. It was also cumbersome. Every one of those installs turned into technical debt: a second site to keep updated and patched, with a theme that had to keep looking like the real one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's where I learned that the developer who builds a site is rarely the person who writes its blog. Usually it's someone in marketing, and sometimes it's the founder at 11pm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


If it's just you, use Markdown
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're the only person writing, and you live in a code editor anyway, you don't need a CMS. You need a folder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Astro content collections handle this well. One config file:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/content.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defineCollection&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'astro:content'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;glob&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'astro/loaders'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'astro/zod'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;blog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineCollection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;loader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;glob&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pattern&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'**/*.md'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;base&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./src/data/blog'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;schema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;pubDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;coerce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;collections&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;blog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Drop Markdown files in &lt;code&gt;src/data/blog&lt;/code&gt;, pull them with &lt;code&gt;getCollection('blog')&lt;/code&gt;, and you have a blog. Not on Astro? Eleventy, Hugo, and Jekyll have been doing this for years. Hat tip to all of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every post is a commit and a deploy. If you're already deploying the site yourself, that's just how you publish, and your posts get versioned with the rest of your code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If this is you, you can close this tab. You're set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


If other people write, they need an editor
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The moment someone outside the dev team writes, Markdown in a repo gets awkward. They need Git access. They break the front matter with one stray colon. They want to see the post before it goes live.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So you need an editor. There are a lot of ways to get one, and they mostly differ on where the content lives:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Examples&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where content lives&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What you still build&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Good when&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Git-based CMS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Decap, Sveltia, Keystatic, TinaCMS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Markdown or JSON files in your repo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Templates and SEO pages, and publishing is still a commit&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You want posts versioned in your repo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Self-hosted headless CMS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Payload, Strapi, Directus&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your own database&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Templates and SEO pages, plus running the CMS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You want to own the data and host it yourself&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hosted headless CMS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sanity, Contentful&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The vendor's cloud&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Templates and SEO pages&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You don't want to run anything (both have free plans)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Classic CMS used headless&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;WordPress (REST API), Ghost (Content API)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Its own database&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Templates and SEO pages, plus running the CMS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your team already uses WordPress or Ghost&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Blog layer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DropInBlog&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The vendor's cloud&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Very little (more on that below)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You're adding a blog to a site that's already built&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All of these are real options. Plenty of good blogs run on each. Payload comes up more than anything else in dev threads right now, and I get why.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But look at the "What you still build" column. With every headless option, the CMS gives you content and an editor. Your app still needs the rest: the post template, the list with pagination, category and author pages, the sitemap, the RSS feed, and the meta tags and schema on every page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you already have Sanity, Payload, or something else for the rest of the site, that's fine. A lot of teams keep that CMS and use DropInBlog for the blog, because the blog stays compartmentalized from the rest of the site, and it's already done. Since blogs are all we do, we can go deep on them, instead of the blog being an afterthought in a much larger system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The difference is our &lt;a href="https://dev.dropinblog.com/reference/api-reference?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=headless-cms-blog" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Rendered API&lt;/a&gt;. Instead of JSON you have to turn into templates, it returns the finished HTML: the post, the list, categories, authors, the sitemap, the feed, the meta tags. You don't design those pieces yourself. When we ship a new blog feature, it shows up for your marketing team without a frontend pass.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What teams get wrong when they add a blog
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The teams that find us usually show up after one of three things: a site migration, a blog nobody wants to touch anymore, or an SEO problem.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most teams build the post page and call it done. Then the requests start: author pages, categories, a redirect because someone changed a slug. Every one lands in the dev backlog, and the blog becomes a second product nobody planned to maintain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For us, the hardest part has always been everything around the posts. Matching each site's design. Keeping every page crawlable and indexable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Astro, our integration handles that part. Install it and add your keys:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm install @dropinblog/astro
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# .env
DROPINBLOG_BLOG_ID=your_dropinblog_blog_id
DROPINBLOG_API_KEY=your_dropinblog_api_key
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then one entry in your config:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// astro.config.mjs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'astro/config'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dropInBlog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'@dropinblog/astro'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;defineConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;site&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'https://yoursite.com'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;integrations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;dropInBlog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That registers the blog, category, and author routes, plus a sitemap, RSS feeds, and JSON-LD on every page. It works in static, server-rendered, or Server Islands mode. On a static build, point our build hook at your host's deploy hook. Every publish triggers a rebuild that skips the posts that didn't change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your marketing team writes in our editor. Nobody opens a pull request to fix a typo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An AI assistant can do the same work through our &lt;a href="https://dev.dropinblog.com/reference/mcp-server?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=headless-cms-blog" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MCP server&lt;/a&gt;. Posts come out the way the editor writes them, so they stay easy to open and edit by hand. SEO analysis is the same 0-100 score the panel already shows. Team permissions apply there too: the assistant can only do what that person's role allows in the editor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When you shouldn't use us
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's when DropInBlog is the wrong pick:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You're the only writer and happy in Markdown.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You're a creator or a newsletter-first publication. There are tools built for that, and we aren't one of them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You want something free. We're paid, with a free trial.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want the full setup, we have an &lt;a href="https://github.com/DropInBlog/astro" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Astro package&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://docs.dropinblog.com/en/article/developer-docs-sdks-and-api-1fqwo39/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=headless-cms-blog" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;packages for other frameworks&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's your blog stack, and who actually writes on it?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg2nj6xk5qodr8dylumfq.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg2nj6xk5qodr8dylumfq.png" alt=" " width="800" height="1421"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Most "free" expense trackers are bloated with loan ads and slow forms. Here is how I built a lightweight, ad-free PWA with automated 11:00 PM IST email audits.&lt;br&gt;
tags: webdev, react, firebase, showdev&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


cover_image: 
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most "free" expense tracking apps on the Play Store and App Store share the same problem: &lt;strong&gt;bloat and ad spam&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You open the app just to log a ₹20 cup of chai or a ₹150 auto ride, and you are hit with full-screen personal loan banners, credit card pop-ups, and multi-step forms. After three days, you stop logging altogether.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To solve this for my own daily spending, I built &lt;strong&gt;Daily House Expense Tracker Pro&lt;/strong&gt;—a lightweight, 100% ad-free Progressive Web App (PWA) designed to log micro-spends in under &lt;strong&gt;3 seconds&lt;/strong&gt; and send an automated summary email every night.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🔗 &lt;strong&gt;Try it live (no app store download needed):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://daily-house-expense-trackerpro.firebaseapp.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Daily House Expense Tracker Pro&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


What Makes It Different?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero Ads, Zero Loan Spam, 100% Free:&lt;/strong&gt; Built purely as a utility tool at Zunaro Labs. No trackers, no banner ads, and no forced bank-account linking.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;3-Second Micro-Spend Logging:&lt;/strong&gt; Open the PWA from your home screen, enter the amount and category (Chai &amp;amp; Snacks, Groceries, Transit, Utilities), and save immediately.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Automated 11:00 PM IST Nightly Email Ledger:&lt;/strong&gt; Every night at 11:00 PM IST, an automated job reconciles your daytime inflows and outflows and emails a clean daily statement straight to your inbox.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Installable PWA (0MB Bloat):&lt;/strong&gt; Works directly in Chrome or Safari and installs to your mobile home screen like a native wallet app.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Instant Financial Statements:&lt;/strong&gt; Filter between variable daily habits and fixed monthly overheads, and export clean reports whenever you need them.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


The Tech Stack &amp;amp; Architecture
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I kept the stack lean so the app loads in under a second on mobile networks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Frontend:&lt;/strong&gt; React + Vite + Tailwind CSS&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Authentication &amp;amp; Database:&lt;/strong&gt; Firebase Auth (1-click Google Sign-In) &amp;amp; Cloud Firestore for real-time sync&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hosting:&lt;/strong&gt; Firebase Hosting&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Automated Nightly Audits:&lt;/strong&gt; GitHub Actions cron workflows (&lt;code&gt;11:00 PM IST&lt;/code&gt; daily cutoff reconciliation &amp;amp; email dispatch)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


What's Next?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I am currently rolling out improvements based on early user feedback—including faster category shortcuts and monthly surplus visual breakdowns.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are tired of clunky finance apps, give it a spin and let me know what feature you would add next in the comments!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;👉 &lt;strong&gt;Launch App:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://daily-house-expense-trackerpro.firebaseapp.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://daily-house-expense-trackerpro.firebaseapp.com/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This article walks through what changed between A2UI 0.9 and A2UI 1.0, how generated UI works on 1.0, and why it is better, using one currency agent built on both versions. A Google ADK agent on Gemini looks up exchange rates and answers with A2UI cards and charts, rendered by a Next.js CopilotKit front end over AG-UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/xbill9/currency-claude-agui-a2ui1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/xbill9/currency-claude-agui-a2ui1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 0.9 the model wrote the UI as JSON, figures included. On 1.0 it writes one line and the server does the rest.&lt;/strong&gt; A rate card for "Convert 100 USD to EUR" is 223 tokens of 0.9 JSON, 139 tokens of 1.0 Express, and 21 tokens as a 1.0 server-side macro call. A 90-day history chart is 1,060 tokens of 0.9 JSON and 21 as a macro call. Counted with the Gemini tokenizer, for the same components.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


What Is A2UI?
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A2UI is a protocol for agents that answer with UI. The agent sends a description of components (cards, text, buttons, charts) from a catalog the client implements, and the client renders them with its own native widgets. The agent never sends code, so the client decides what can run.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


The Agent Used for the Comparison
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fm2ouwuq62ys7qdj3et4s.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fm2ouwuq62ys7qdj3et4s.png" alt="Architecture: the browser talks AG-UI to a bridge on port 8008, A2A clients talk to a server on port 10000, both run the same ADK agent, which calls the MCP server on port 8080 and the Frankfurter API" width="800" height="180"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Service&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Port&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Code&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React UI: Next.js, CopilotKit, &lt;code&gt;@a2ui/react&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;frontend-react/&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;AG-UI bridge: the ADK agent in-process&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8008&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;frontend-react/agent/&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A2A server: the same ADK agent over A2A&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;currency_agent/&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MCP server: &lt;code&gt;get_exchange_rate&lt;/code&gt; (FastMCP)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8080, path &lt;code&gt;/mcp&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;mcp-server/&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 0.9 build is commit &lt;code&gt;d181ddb&lt;/code&gt; of the same repository. Everything after it is 1.0. Versions, read from the machine on 2026-11-11:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Component&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;0.9 build&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;1.0 build&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Agent library&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;a2ui-agent-sdk&lt;/code&gt; 0.2.1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;a2ui-agent-sdk&lt;/code&gt; 0.11.0, &lt;code&gt;a2ui-adk&lt;/code&gt; 0.3.0, &lt;code&gt;a2ui-core&lt;/code&gt; 0.6.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Renderer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;@copilotkit/a2ui-renderer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;@a2ui/react&lt;/code&gt; 0.15.0, &lt;code&gt;@a2ui/web_core&lt;/code&gt; 0.15.0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Agent framework&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;google-adk&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;google-adk&lt;/code&gt; 2.13.0, &lt;code&gt;ag_ui_adk&lt;/code&gt; 0.6.5&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Gemini&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;gemini-3.8-flash&lt;/code&gt; (default), &lt;code&gt;gemini-2.5-flash&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


What Changed at a Glance
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read from the installed &lt;code&gt;a2ui-core&lt;/code&gt; 0.6.0, which ships the 0.9 and 1.0 schemas side by side:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;A2UI 0.9&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;A2UI 1.0&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Version string&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;"v0.9"&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;"1.0"&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;createSurface&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;catalog id, &lt;code&gt;theme&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sendDataModel&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;inline &lt;code&gt;components&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;dataModel&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sendDataModel&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;metadata&lt;/code&gt;; no &lt;code&gt;theme&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Catalogs per surface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;one&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;one per component, through &lt;code&gt;catalogId&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Bindings and calls&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;{"path": ...}&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;{"call": ...}&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;{"@path": ...}&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;{"@call": ...}&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Agent → renderer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;createSurface, updateComponents, updateDataModel, deleteSurface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the same four, plus callRendererFunction and agentFunctionResponse&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Renderer → agent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;action, error&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;action, error, plus callAgentFunction and rendererFunctionResponse&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text variants&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, caption, body&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;caption, body; headings come from Markdown&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Basic catalog&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;18 components, 14 functions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the same 18 components, 15 functions (adds &lt;code&gt;@index&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Names for the two sides&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;client and server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;renderer and agent&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What the model writes (SDK)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;JSON&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Express, compiled to JSON on the server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Server-side macros (SDK)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;none&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;@macro&lt;/code&gt;, expanded before sending&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The last two rows belong to the agent SDK, &lt;code&gt;a2ui-agent-sdk&lt;/code&gt; 0.11.0, which ships with the 1.0 protocol. The sections below take the changes one at a time: what 0.9 did, what 1.0 does, and what it buys.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Change 1 — A Whole Surface in One Message
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 0.9&lt;/strong&gt; a new surface took three messages: &lt;code&gt;createSurface&lt;/code&gt; named the catalog, &lt;code&gt;updateComponents&lt;/code&gt; sent the components, and &lt;code&gt;updateDataModel&lt;/code&gt; filled in the data. The renderer held an empty surface until the last one arrived.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 1.0&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;createSurface&lt;/code&gt; carries &lt;code&gt;components&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;dataModel&lt;/code&gt; inline. This Express block, a form with a bound text field, a check and a button, compiles to both versions with the SDK's own compiler:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;surface("main")
$/user/name = "Ada"
title = Text(formatString("Hello ${/user/name}"))
btn = Button(Text("Save"), action=Event("save", {name: $/user/name}, userMessage="Saving"))
root = Column([title, TextField("Name", $/user/name, checks=[?required("Name is required")]), btn])
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python3 docs/a2ui-1-0/tools/express_0_9_vs_1_0.py
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;## A2UI 1.0: 1 message(s): ['createSurface']
## A2UI 0.9 (userMessage removed): 3 message(s): ['createSurface', 'updateComponents', 'updateDataModel']
## userMessage on 0.9
ExpressValidationError: Event userMessage requires protocol v1.0 or later; the target version v0.9 has no userMessage field.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it is better:&lt;/strong&gt; the renderer has everything it needs to draw the surface from the first message, and there is no ordering between three messages to get wrong. Updates still use &lt;code&gt;updateComponents&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;updateDataModel&lt;/code&gt; once a surface exists.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Change 2 — The Model Writes Express, Not JSON
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 0.9&lt;/strong&gt; the model wrote A2UI JSON into its reply, inside &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a2ui-json&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags. The 0.9 build's &lt;code&gt;page.tsx&lt;/code&gt; cut the blocks out of the text with regular expressions, and a &lt;code&gt;normalizeA2UIJson&lt;/code&gt; function repaired the shapes the model produced before handing them to the renderer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 1.0&lt;/strong&gt; the SDK's default output format is Express: a short Python-like language inside &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a2ui&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags. &lt;code&gt;$/path&lt;/code&gt; is a binding, &lt;code&gt;?required&lt;/code&gt; is a check, &lt;code&gt;root =&lt;/code&gt; names the top component, and arguments follow the catalog's signatures. The SDK generates the prompt from the catalogs, compiles the block on the server, validates it against the catalog, and sends the JSON. Here is the rate card in Express:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;surface("main")
root__text_1 = Text("### 100.00 USD = 89.87 EUR")
root__text_2 = Text("1 USD = 0.89871 EUR")
root__divider_1 = Divider()
root__text_3 = Text("ECB reference rate for 2026-11-11 (Frankfurter API)", "caption")
root__column_1 = Column([root__text_1, root__text_2, root__divider_1, root__text_3])
root = Card(root__column_1)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The same six components as 0.9 JSON:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;[{"version":"v0.9","createSurface":{"surfaceId":"main","catalogId":"https://a2ui.org/catalog/currency-charts"}},{"version":"v0.9","updateComponents":{"surfaceId":"main","components":[{"component":"Text","id":"root__text_1","text":"100.00 USD = 89.87 EUR","variant":"h3"},{"component":"Text","id":"root__text_2","text":"1 USD = 0.89871 EUR"},{"component":"Divider","id":"root__divider_1"},{"component":"Text","id":"root__text_3","text":"ECB reference rate for 2026-11-11 (Frankfurter API)","variant":"caption"},{"component":"Column","id":"root__column_1","children":["root__text_1","root__text_2","root__divider_1","root__text_3"]},{"component":"Card","id":"root","child":"root__column_1"}]}}]
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it is better:&lt;/strong&gt; the model writes fewer tokens (139 against 223 for this card, table below), and a malformed block fails on the server, before anything reaches a browser: the tool returns the compiler's error to the model in its result. The browser receives JSON that has already passed the catalog's schema, so the 1.0 &lt;code&gt;page.tsx&lt;/code&gt; has no parser and no normalizer: it went from 309 lines to 150.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Change 3 — Server-Side Macros: Figures Never Pass Through the Model
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 0.9&lt;/strong&gt; the model called the rate tool, read the result, and typed every figure into the component JSON. A history chart meant the model copying 64 dates and 64 rates back out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 1.0&lt;/strong&gt; a macro is a Python function the model calls like a component. &lt;code&gt;@macro&lt;/code&gt; turns its type hints and docstring into an entry in a separate macros catalog that only the model sees. At send time &lt;code&gt;MacroExpander&lt;/code&gt; runs the function and replaces the call with Basic catalog components, so the browser needs no macro support. From &lt;code&gt;currency_agent/macros.py&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@macro&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RateCard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currency_from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;currency_to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;amount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;1.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"""Card with the live exchange rate and a converted amount. The server looks
up the rate, so do not pass rate figures.
"""&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dst&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;_code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currency_from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;_code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;currency_to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;_get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"latest"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"from"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;src&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"to"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;dst&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;rate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rates"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;dst&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Card&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;child&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Column&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"### {amount:,.2f} {src} = {amount * rate:,.2f} {dst}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"1 {src} = {rate:.5f} {dst}"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Divider&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ECB reference rate for {data['date']} (Frankfurter API)"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;variant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"caption"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For "Convert 100 USD to EUR please." the model's whole tool call was:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a2ui&amp;gt;
surface("conversion")
root = RateCard("USD", "EUR", 100)
&amp;lt;/a2ui&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;What reached the browser:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;version 1.0, ['createSurface'], 6 components
https://a2ui.org/specification/v1_0/catalogs/basic/catalog.json: ['Text', 'Text', 'Divider', 'Text', 'Column', 'Card']
Text: ### 100.00 USD = 89.87 EUR
Text: 1 USD = 0.89871 EUR
Text: ECB reference rate for 2026-11-11 (Frankfurter API)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The agent validates twice: once against the catalogs the model sees, which include the macros, and again after expansion against the catalogs the browser implements:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;_parse_payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;self&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;processor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;super&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;_parse_payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;processor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;typed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;_message_adapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;validate_python&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;macro_expander&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;transform_to_transport&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;typed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;wire&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;model_dump&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;by_alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;exclude_none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;m&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;expanded&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;validate_payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BASE_CATALOGS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wire&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;wire&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it is better:&lt;/strong&gt; the figures come from the source on the server, so the model cannot mistype a rate, and data the model does not need to see stays out of its context. The model's output stops growing with the data: the 90-day chart is 21 tokens as a macro call against 1,060 as 0.9 JSON. Macro arguments come from the model, so &lt;code&gt;_code()&lt;/code&gt; accepts only a three-letter currency code and day counts are clamped to 7–365.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwc1ncjb6xlly4n6bgm61.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fwc1ncjb6xlly4n6bgm61.png" alt="Line chart of USD to JPY from 2026-08-13 to 2026-11-11 with low 153.27, high 160.16 and latest 159.23" width="800" height="760"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


How Many Tokens Does the Model Write?
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;docs/a2ui-1-0/tools/token_cost.py&lt;/code&gt; builds three answers once with live rates, writes each in four forms, and counts every form with the Gemini &lt;code&gt;countTokens&lt;/code&gt; API:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python3 docs/a2ui-1-0/tools/token_cost.py gemini-2.5-flash
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# Tokens the model writes for the same UI, counted by gemini-2.5-flash countTokens
| Answer | Components | 0.9 JSON | 1.0 JSON | 1.0 Express | 1.0 macro |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Rate card | 6 | 223 | 212 | 139 | 21 |
| 90-day chart | 5 | 1060 | 1152 | 1101 | 21 |
| 3-currency comparison | 8 | 347 | 522 | 253 | 22 |
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;gemini-3.8-flash&lt;/code&gt; counted the same numbers. Two things the table shows:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Express shrinks structure.&lt;/strong&gt; On the card and the comparison, where most tokens are component structure, Express is the smallest form the model can write by hand. On the chart, where most tokens are 64 dates and 64 rates, every hand-written form costs about the same.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Macros remove the data.&lt;/strong&gt; A macro call costs the same 21 or 22 tokens whatever it expands to.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 1.0 JSON for the comparison is the largest form because it mixes two catalogs, so each component names its own &lt;code&gt;catalogId&lt;/code&gt; (Change 4). Express leaves catalog ids out and the compiler fills them in.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Change 4 — Several Catalogs on One Surface
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 0.9&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;createSurface&lt;/code&gt; named one catalog id, and every component on the surface came from it. The 0.9 build loaded Basic 0.9 and its charts catalog, &lt;code&gt;https://a2ui.org/catalog/currency-charts&lt;/code&gt;, through &lt;code&gt;A2uiSchemaManager&lt;/code&gt;, which selected one catalog for the surface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 1.0&lt;/strong&gt; each component can name its own &lt;code&gt;catalogId&lt;/code&gt;. The browser here implements Basic 1.0 and a currency charts catalog with &lt;code&gt;LineChart&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;BarChart&lt;/code&gt;. For "Show me EUR to USD for the past 6 months and the current rate" the model wrote two macro calls:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a2ui&amp;gt;
surface("main")
root = Column([rateCard, historyChart])
rateCard = RateCard("EUR", "USD")
historyChart = RateHistoryChart("EUR", "USD", 180)
&amp;lt;/a2ui&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The browser received one &lt;code&gt;createSurface&lt;/code&gt; with each component naming its catalog:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;version 1.0, ['createSurface'], 12 components
https://a2ui.org/specification/v1_0/catalogs/basic/catalog.json: ['Column', 'Text', 'Text', 'Divider', 'Text', 'Column', 'Card', 'Text', 'Text', 'Column', 'Card']
https://a2ui.org/catalog/currency-charts/v1/catalog.json: ['LineChart']
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg1h3ecw97kcbvzdibb5u.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fg1h3ecw97kcbvzdibb5u.png" alt="A rate card for EUR to USD above a 180-day line chart" width="800" height="1019"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it is better:&lt;/strong&gt; a custom catalog holds only the custom components, &lt;code&gt;LineChart&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;BarChart&lt;/code&gt; here, and sits beside the standard Basic catalog on the same surface. The charts catalog is now &lt;code&gt;https://a2ui.org/catalog/currency-charts/v1/catalog.json&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;protocolVersion&lt;/code&gt; 1.0, and its id and props match between &lt;code&gt;currency_agent/custom_catalog.json&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;A2UICustomCatalog.tsx&lt;/code&gt; in the browser.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Change 5 — The UI Arrives as a Tool Result
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 0.9&lt;/strong&gt; the UI travelled inside the reply text. CopilotKit's A2UI middleware and &lt;code&gt;@copilotkit/a2ui-renderer&lt;/code&gt; read it, and the 0.9 build's AG-UI checks searched the final text for &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a2ui-json&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On 1.0&lt;/strong&gt; the model passes its Express block to the &lt;code&gt;send_a2ui_json_to_client&lt;/code&gt; tool, and the tool result carries the validated messages in &lt;code&gt;validated_a2ui_json&lt;/code&gt;. The page registers a renderer for that tool and hands the messages to &lt;code&gt;@a2ui/react&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRenderTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"send_a2ui_json_to_client"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;extractA2uiMessages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;A2uiMessages&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;messages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why it is better:&lt;/strong&gt; the UI travels separately from the chat text, so a reply can mix prose and a card without the browser guessing where one ends. CopilotKit's built-in A2UI middleware speaks 0.9, so it is switched off in &lt;code&gt;route.ts&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fs9odz4whycal1p6rj7bi.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fs9odz4whycal1p6rj7bi.png" alt="1 USD against 3 currencies: today's rates for EUR, GBP and CHF above a bar chart of the change since 2026-10-12, EUR up 0.49 percent, GBP down 0.54, CHF down 0.36" width="800" height="970"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Change 6 — Breaking Changes to Plan For
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These need edits when moving a 0.9 agent or renderer to 1.0:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Headings come from Markdown.&lt;/strong&gt; Text's &lt;code&gt;h1&lt;/code&gt;–&lt;code&gt;h5&lt;/code&gt; variants are gone; &lt;code&gt;caption&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;body&lt;/code&gt; remain, and Text renders Markdown. The macros write &lt;code&gt;### 250.00 USD = 224.68 EUR&lt;/code&gt; for the headline line, and &lt;code&gt;@a2ui/react&lt;/code&gt; renders it with &lt;code&gt;@a2ui/markdown-it&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;theme&lt;/code&gt; is gone from &lt;code&gt;createSurface&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bindings and calls are prefixed:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;{"path": ...}&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;{"@path": ...}&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;{"call": ...}&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;{"@call": ...}&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The version string is &lt;code&gt;"1.0"&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, without the &lt;code&gt;v&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Component props moved:&lt;/strong&gt; TextField gains &lt;code&gt;placeholder&lt;/code&gt; and replaces &lt;code&gt;validationRegexp&lt;/code&gt; with a &lt;code&gt;regex&lt;/code&gt; check, Slider gains &lt;code&gt;steps&lt;/code&gt;, and Video gains &lt;code&gt;posterUrl&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Change 7 — What 1.0 Adds
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From the 1.0 schemas in &lt;code&gt;a2ui-core&lt;/code&gt; 0.6.0:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Functions in both directions.&lt;/strong&gt; The agent can call a renderer function (&lt;code&gt;callRendererFunction&lt;/code&gt;) and the renderer can call an agent function (&lt;code&gt;callAgentFunction&lt;/code&gt;), each with its own response message.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Function permissions.&lt;/strong&gt; A catalog marks each function &lt;code&gt;rendererOnly&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;agentOnly&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;rendererOrAgent&lt;/code&gt;, and can require a user gesture with &lt;code&gt;requiresUserActivation&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Composition rules.&lt;/strong&gt; A catalog can list &lt;code&gt;allowedParents&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;allowedChildren&lt;/code&gt; per component, and the renderer reports violations with the error codes &lt;code&gt;UNALLOWED_PARENT&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;UNALLOWED_CHILD&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Graded validation.&lt;/strong&gt; Checks return a result with a &lt;code&gt;severity&lt;/code&gt; of error, warning or info.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Actions that speak.&lt;/strong&gt; A button's action can carry a &lt;code&gt;userMessage&lt;/code&gt;, which travels with the action to the agent. The compiler rejects it on 0.9, as Change 1 shows.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Accessibility.&lt;/strong&gt; Components take &lt;code&gt;live&lt;/code&gt; (off, polite, assertive) and &lt;code&gt;hidden&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;List positions.&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;code&gt;@index&lt;/code&gt; function returns an item's position inside a list template.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Extensions.&lt;/strong&gt; Components, surfaces, actions and catalogs carry &lt;code&gt;metadata.extensions&lt;/code&gt; for custom data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Catalog instructions.&lt;/strong&gt; A catalog can carry Markdown &lt;code&gt;instructions&lt;/code&gt; for the model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Does It Hold Up?
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkm49gmbyjfctzebrov8h.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fkm49gmbyjfctzebrov8h.png" alt="Before and after: on A2UI 0.9 the model calls the rate tool and writes the component JSON with the figures, the reply text carries a2ui-json blocks, and the browser cuts them out and repairs them; on A2UI 1.0 the model writes one macro call, the server compiles and validates it, the macro fetches the rate, the result is validated again and arrives as a tool result" width="798" height="238"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;docs/a2ui-1-0/tools/render_suite.py&lt;/code&gt; sends ten prompts ten times each through Next.js. A UI prompt passes when the reply rendered A2UI and no raw &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;a2ui&lt;/code&gt; text reached the chat; the weather question passes as text with no UI.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;python3 docs/a2ui-1-0/tools/render_suite.py 10 gemini-3.8-flash render-suite-gemini-3.8-flash.jsonl
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# gemini-3.8-flash: 100 of 100 replies rendered clean (10 runs per prompt)
10 of 10: pass | Compare 1 USD in EUR, GBP, JPY and CAD
10 of 10: pass | Compare the euro, pound and Swiss franc against the dollar in a bar chart
10 of 10: pass | Convert 100 USD to XYZ
10 of 10: pass | How much is 250 USD in EUR?
10 of 10: pass | Show a bar chart of 1 USD in EUR, GBP, JPY, CHF and CAD
10 of 10: pass | Show me EUR to USD for the past 6 months and the current rate
10 of 10: pass | Show me the USD to JPY trend over the last 90 days
10 of 10: pass | What's a thousand euros in dollars?
10 of 10: pass | What's the weather in Paris?
10 of 10: pass | Write the A2UI block for a USD to EUR rate card directly in your reply text. Do not call any tool.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;# gemini-2.5-flash: 100 of 100 replies rendered clean (10 runs per prompt)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Every comparison reply on both models came from the &lt;code&gt;RateComparison&lt;/code&gt; macro, with no call to the rate tool:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;## gemini-2.5-flash
10 of 10: no rate-tool call, BarChart=True | Compare 1 USD in EUR, GBP, JPY and CAD
10 of 10: no rate-tool call, BarChart=True | Compare the euro, pound and Swiss franc against the dol
10 of 10: no rate-tool call, BarChart=True | Show a bar chart of 1 USD in EUR, GBP, JPY, CHF and CAD
## gemini-3.8-flash
10 of 10: no rate-tool call, BarChart=True | Compare 1 USD in EUR, GBP, JPY and CAD
10 of 10: no rate-tool call, BarChart=True | Compare the euro, pound and Swiss franc against the dol
10 of 10: no rate-tool call, BarChart=True | Show a bar chart of 1 USD in EUR, GBP, JPY, CHF and CAD
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h4&gt;


🔎 Tip: Express Is a Format, Not a Python Feature
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The A2UI repository ships YAML conformance suites for the Express compiler and the response parser, tagged with the &lt;code&gt;a2ui-agent-sdk&lt;/code&gt; 0.11.0 release. A Rust port of the same agent, &lt;a href="https://github.com/xbill9/currency-agent-rust-agui-a2ui1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/xbill9/currency-agent-rust-agui-a2ui1&lt;/a&gt;, implements Express and server-side macros in Rust and runs them:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;cargo &lt;span class="nb"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt; --test express_conformance -- --nocapture
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;agent/express/response_parser.yaml: 38/38 passed, 0 known gaps, 0 failed
agent/express/compiler.yaml: 132/135 passed, 3 known gaps, 0 failed
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The three compiler cases it skips use a 0.9 catalog; the port targets 1.0 only.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


🔎 Tip: Keep the Macros Catalog on the Model's Side
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The macros sit in a catalog that exists only for the model: &lt;code&gt;macro_expander.transform_catalogs(BASE_CATALOGS)&lt;/code&gt; adds it to the prompt and the compiler. The browser's catalogs stay Basic 1.0 plus charts, and the second validation pass confirms every component the browser receives belongs to one of them.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


At This Point You Should Have…
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Python 3.13 or newer and Node.js with npm&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A Gemini API key from Google AI Studio, or a Google Cloud project with Vertex AI enabled&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The repository cloned:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;git clone https://github.com/xbill9/currency-claude-agui-a2ui1.git
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd&lt;/span&gt; currency-claude-agui-a2ui1
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h4&gt;


Step 1 — Install and Configure
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;make install
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"GOOGLE_API_KEY=&amp;lt;your_api_key_here&amp;gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; .env
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"GOOGLE_GENAI_USE_VERTEXAI=FALSE"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; .env
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;make install&lt;/code&gt; runs &lt;code&gt;uv sync&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;npm install&lt;/code&gt; for both front ends. The agent uses &lt;code&gt;gemini-3.8-flash&lt;/code&gt; unless &lt;code&gt;GENAI_MODEL&lt;/code&gt; is set in &lt;code&gt;.env&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Step 2 — Start the Stack and Ask
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;make start
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:10000/health
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8008/health
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;{"status":"ok"}
{"status":"ok"}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;a href="http://localhost:3000" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;http://localhost:3000&lt;/a&gt; and ask &lt;strong&gt;How much is 250 USD in EUR?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffjhttf83628hs220ovsa.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ffjhttf83628hs220ovsa.png" alt="Rate card: 250.00 USD = 224.68 EUR, 1 USD = 0.89871 EUR, ECB reference rate for 2026-11-11" width="800" height="421"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Compare and Contrast
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same answers on each version, tokens counted by &lt;code&gt;gemini-2.5-flash&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;A2UI 0.9&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;A2UI 1.0, Express&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;A2UI 1.0, macro 🥇&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rate card&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;223 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;139 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;21 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;90-day chart&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,060 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,101 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;21 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3-currency comparison&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;347 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;253 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;22 tokens&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Messages per new surface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Figures typed by&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the model&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;the server&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Checked against the catalog&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;no; shapes repaired in the browser&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;on the server, before sending&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;on the server, before and after expansion&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Catalogs per surface&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1 per component&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1 per component&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


So, Which One?
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Move to 1.0. A surface arrives in one message, the model writes Express instead of JSON, the server validates before anything ships, and one surface can mix the standard Basic catalog with your own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 1.0, write a macro for anything the agent shows often. The model's output drops to one line with the codes and an amount, the figures come from the source on the server, and the cost no longer grows with the data. Rate cards, history charts and comparisons all take that path here. Plain Express covers layouts the macros do not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Budget time for the breaking changes in Change 6, and for a renderer that speaks 1.0: &lt;code&gt;@a2ui/react&lt;/code&gt; 0.15.0 here, in place of CopilotKit's 0.9 renderer.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h4&gt;


Summary
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The goal of this article was to show what changed between A2UI 0.9 and A2UI 1.0, how generated UI works on 1.0, and why it is better, using one currency agent built on both. The key to the solution was the 1.0 agent SDK's two additions on top of the protocol: the model writes Express that the server compiles and validates, and server-side macros build the components from live data so the figures never pass through the model. The results were:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 A rate card is 223 tokens of 0.9 JSON, 139 tokens of Express and 21 tokens as a macro call, counted by the Gemini tokenizer&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 A 90-day chart is 1,060 tokens of 0.9 JSON and 21 as a macro call; Express alone saves little when the tokens are data&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 The same Express block compiles to one message on 1.0 and three on 0.9&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 One surface mixed two catalogs, each component naming its own &lt;code&gt;catalogId&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 Ten prompts, ten runs each: 100 of 100 replies rendered clean on &lt;code&gt;gemini-3.8-flash&lt;/code&gt; and 100 of 100 on &lt;code&gt;gemini-2.5-flash&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 The browser code shrank from 309 lines to 150 once the parser and normalizer went&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🟢 A Rust port passed 132 of 135 Express compiler cases and 38 of 38 response parser cases; the 3 it skips use a 0.9 catalog&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;⚠️ 1.0 JSON that mixes catalogs repeats &lt;code&gt;catalogId&lt;/code&gt; on every component and was the largest form of the comparison, at 522 tokens&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;❌ 1.0 drops &lt;code&gt;theme&lt;/code&gt; and the h1–h5 Text variants, and renames &lt;code&gt;path&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;call&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;@path&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;@call&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scope: one laptop running all four services, &lt;code&gt;gemini-3.8-flash&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;gemini-2.5-flash&lt;/code&gt; at their default settings, ECB reference rates from the Frankfurter API, measured on 2026-11-11 with ten runs per prompt per model. The token counts compare forms of the same components built by the server: the 0.9 JSON is those components written in the 0.9 shape, not text captured from the 0.9 build's model, and properties left at their defaults are removed from every form. Protocol details come from the 0.9 and 1.0 schemas in &lt;code&gt;a2ui-core&lt;/code&gt; 0.6.0.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The strategy for moving a generative UI agent from A2UI 0.9 to 1.0 was validated with an incremental step by step approach.&lt;/p&gt;




